\subsection{Interaction Hub} \label{memory_hub}

In this section, we introduce the Interaction Hub, which implements the prompt interaction strategy. 

% Previous works MaPLe~\cite{khattak2023maple} and PMF~\cite{li2023efficient} reflect Projection strategy of prompt interaction, which perform cross-modality interaction via vanilla MLP projection, without interaction between prompt vectors,as shown in~\figref{fig_appendix} (a). Unlike previous work, we design an InteractionHub for interaction between prompts to capture inter- and intra-modality correlations. Inspired by previous multimodal interaction strategy tensor operation~\cite{zadeh2017tensor, sun2022cubemlp} and attention operation~\cite{nagrani2021attention,chen2020swafn}, we attempted linear strategy and attention strategy for prompt interaction, as shown in~\figref{fig_appendix} (b) and (c). 
% % During the initial attempt, we design a Linear strategy to carry out prompt interaction in InteractionHub. 
% Linear strategy refers to concatenating temporal prompts from both modalities and leverage linear projection on the concatenated vector. However, we found that an excessive number of linear layers not only introduces a greater number of optimization parameters but also results in suboptimal classification performance. Inspired by previous work MBT~\cite{nagrani2021attention} which performs cross-attention on intermediate layers to capture correspondences between modalities, we adopt attention strategy to apply cross-attention between temporal prompts from both modalities in order to capture corresponding information from the other modality. The performance was improved, yet resulted in large quantity of trainable parameters and consumption of GPU memory. Ultimately, we proposed the similarity-based method, EPIC, as shown in~\figref{fig_appendix} (d). Similarity strategy is adopted in the final structure of InteractionHub. The detailed analysis of strategies inside InteractionHub is presented in~\secref{sec:variances}.



We first project $\hat{p}_{m'}^{l}$ into the space of $\hat{p}_{m}^{l}$ through a two-layer MLP, denoted as $\tilde p_{m'}^{l}$. We consider two types of correspondences between temporal prompts: those intra-modality and those inter-modality. Analogical mapping connection is leveraged to discover correspondences between tokens, working as $Mapping(A,B)=f_{M}(sim(A,B))$, where $f_{M}$ means the mapping function, and $sim$ means similarity function. Specifically, the correspondence mapping connections are calculated as:
\begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
        F_{m}^{intra} & = ReLU(sim(MLP(\hat{p}_{m}^{l}),\hat{p}_{m}^{l})), \\
        F_{m}^{inter} & = ReLU(sim(\hat{p}_{m}^{l}, \tilde p_{m'}^{l})).
        \label{XX4}
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}

