    % For comparison, we implement several baseline models on our employed datasets and report the results for comparison. 
    Our baseline models are as follows:\textbf{ a) }Pre-trained LMM foundation model with late fusion module, CMA-CLIP~\cite{fu2022cma}. \textbf{b)} Pre-trained LMM foundation model with prompt-tuning, MaPLe~\cite{khattak2023maple}. We re-implement MaPLe under our experimental settings on our employed image-text classification datasets. We also propose a prompt-tuning CLIP without interactions between modalities, denoted as P-CLIP.
    % , to compare our proposed method with prompt-tuning strategy. In P-CLIP, we implement deep prompt-tuning on both vision and text branches, with the pre-trained foundation model in frozen. 
    \textbf{c)} Existing prompt-based methods for multimodal interaction, PromptFuse~\cite{liang2022modular}, BlindPrompt~\cite{liang2022modular}, and PMF~\cite{li2023efficient} . \textbf{d)} Late fusion methods with a fusion module, \cite{narayana2019huse,liang2021multibench,hu2021unit,li1908visualbert,xue2023dynamic}.

    % By experimental results, we demonstrate that EPIC present good performance on diverse image-text classification types, including uni-label classification with complementary information from image and text (UPMC-Food101), multi-label classification (MM-IMDB) and semantic relationship deduction (SNLI-VE). Furthermore, EPIC is efficient in terms of trainable parameters, GPU memory usage and time cost in training, while it achieves the best performance on UPMC-Food101 and SNLI-VE datasets, as well as comparable performance on MM-IMDB dataset. 



    \textbf{EPIC is effective on diverse image-text classification task types.} 
    % To demonstrate the effect of our method, we compare EPIC with various existing late fusion strategies, prompt-tuning strategies and prompt-based interaction strategies, including CMA-CLIP~\cite{fu2022cma}, MMBT~\cite{kiela2019supervised}, PromptFuse~\cite{liang2022modular}, PMF~\cite{li2023efficient}, etc. The results of our method are shown in~\tabref{tab:my_label1}. EPIC demonstrates effectiveness on all three datasets for diverse image-text task types. 
    From the results, we observe that our proposed method yields superior results on uni-label classification dataset UPMC-Food101 and multimodal visual entailment dataset SNLI-VE, as well as comparable results on multi-label classification dataset MM-IMDB. In detail, we can achieve an accuracy of 93.95\(\%\) on UPMC-Food101, F1-micro/F1-macro of 65.9/56.3 on MM-IMDB, and an accuracy of 73.45\(\%\) on SNLI-VE. In particular, we outperform all existing prompt-based methods on datasets UPMC-Food101 and SNLI-VE. The small version of our proposed method (EPIC-small) also achieves competitive results on all three datasets. 

    % Exploring the internal structure of the InteractionHub, similarity-SoftMax mechanism acts similar to attention mechanism. Inspired by attention mechanism, we suppose similarity score between two prompt vectors to implicate importance of prompt vector logits. Therefore, we re-weight the prompt vector logits according to similarity score which is passed through a SoftMax layer, such that important logits of temporal prompts are highlighted. This allows the temporal prompt vector to explore inter- and intra-modality correlations. Then we blend re-weighted temporal prompts from both modality according to gate weight $z_m^l$ to produce temporal prompts for the next layer, such that each produced prompt contains both information from current modality and complementary information from the other modality. Through this strategy, both inter- and intra-modality correlations are considered, and thus provides more sufficient modality interaction.


    \textbf{EPIC is efficient in terms of trainable parameters, and GPU memory usage.} We compare the efficiency our proposed method EPIC with other prompt-based methods, the overall comparison is presented in~\tabref{tab:my_label1} and~\figref{fig5}. 
    % The efficiency is considered from perspectives of the number of trainable parameters, the maximum memory usage in training. We present comparisons of  efficiency of parameters and memory usage in~\figref{fig5}. 
    EPIC achieves the second highest accuracy on UPMC-Food101 with only 2.0M parameters (about 1\(\%\) of its pre-trained foundation model) to update and a minimal memory usage in training. 
    % We also compare the training time of each epoch, inference time per sample, and GFLOPs of our proposed method EPIC and other prompt-based methods. As shown in~\tabref{Table8}, EPIC is the fastest in training and the second fastest in inference, with slight GFLOPs increase compared to baseline model. The illustrations show that EPIC reaches best performance among prompt-based methods (competitive results among all terms of methods) with minimal cost in resource and time for training.


    % With an insight into EPIC, information of extracted features is stored in temporal prompts on intermediate layers, and two-way information exchange is conducted through prompt interaction, without participation of extracted features. Prompt vectors contain much lower dimensions than extracted features, reducing spatial complexity of inter-modality information exchange. Moreover, calculative similarity-based prompt interaction mechanism ensures effective information exchange without increasing trainable parameters or model complexity. Comparing to fine-tuning methods with fine-tuning prompts leveraged on multiple layers from shallower ones to deeper ones, EPIC leverages temporal prompts on selected deeper layers, significantly reduces the memory usage.

