\subsection*{Materials and Methods}

\subsubsection*{Observational data\label{sec:meth1}}

WASP-19b was observed by JWST during cycle 1 as part of the GTO program $\#$1274 (PI: J Lunine). The observation, carried out on February 18, 2023, used NIRSpec/PRISM \cite{2022A&A...661A..80J} in the Bright Object Time Series (BOTS) \cite{2023PASP..135a8002E} mode. This set-up enables low-resolution time-series spectroscopy across a broad wavelength range of $\sim$0.6–5.2$\mu$m. However, such lower resolution tends to saturate moderately brighter objects quickly, making it unsuitable for follow-up observations of a large number of bright sources. Given the relatively faint source of WASP-19b, which prevents immediate saturation using NIRSpec/PRISM, this setup allowed for broader wavelength coverage in a single transit. We accessed the publicly available data from these observations using the Barbara A. Mikulski Archive for Space Telescopes (MAST)\footnote{https://mast.stsci.edu/}. The observations span roughly 5.8 hours, covering the entire secondary eclipse of WASP-19b, along with a significant out-of-eclipse baseline. Complete coverage of the secondary eclipse event is crucial to model the baseline trends accurately, particularly for highly sensitive techniques like emission spectroscopy.

\subsubsection*{Data reduction\label{sec:meth2}}

The official archive for JWST provides data at various stages of reduction from the JWST Science Calibration Pipeline \cite{2022zndo...7071140B}, ranging from stage 0 to stage 3. The stage 0 files contain the raw data in FITS format (“*uncal.fits"), and consist of four dimensions: the column and row axes of the detector, the number of groups per integration, and the number of integrations per exposure. The data are divided into multiple sectors (e.g., three in our case) to accommodate the large volume. The PRISM observations use the subarray SUB512, which has a spectral extent of 512 pixels on the NRS1 detector.

We have reduced the stage 0 data using two independent pipelines: Eureka! \cite{2022JOSS....7.4503B} and exoTEDRF (formerly supreme-SPOON) \cite{2024JOSS....9.6898R}. Both pipelines are well-adopted by the community and have been explicitly tested (especially Eureka!) for their accuracy through several previously published works (e.g., \cite{2023Natur.614..664A, 2023NatAs...7.1317L, 2024Natur.626..979P, 2023ApJ...959L...9M} for Eureka! and \cite{2024ApJ...962L..20R, 2023Natur.614..670F} for exoTEDRF). Using two independent pipelines in our analyses enables cross-validation of the results, a necessary step in sensitivity-prone studies such as probing exoplanet atmospheres.

Both pipelines offer well-structured data reduction procedures, divided into various stages, each comprising multiple sub-processes essential for optimal reduction of the time-series data. These include key calibration and correction steps, such as bias subtraction, flat-fielding, ramp fitting, and 1/f correction, followed by spectroscopic extraction of the observed data. In addition to the science frames, these reduction pipelines also require calibration frames, which were accessed through the JWST Calibration Reference Data System (CRDS) \footnote{https://jwst-crds.stsci.edu/}. We closely followed the official documentation of these pipelines, mostly adopting the recommended parameter settings from previous work. The data extraction stages resulted in 2-dimensional lightcurves, resolved both in the time and wavelength axes.

\subsubsection*{Lightcurves Modeling \label{sec:meth3}}

Given the lower resolution of the spectra obtained from NIRSPEC/PRISM compared to the higher resolution grism observations, we are limited in our flexibility to choose an optimal wavelength binning scheme. However, after testing different binning schemes, we have adopted a relatively higher possible resolution, with 200 near-equally spaced bins, allowing for more than two pixels of spectral data per bin on average. Such higher resolution enables us to analyze subtle spectroscopic features without excessively compromising on the uncertainties. Additionally, since the emission spectral features are much more pronounced than the transmission spectra, especially for UHJs, larger uncertainties do not significantly affect our atmospheric interpretation.

To model the 1D lightcurves, we developed a new pipeline named ExoELF (ExoplanEts Lightcurves Fitter). This pipeline integrates several open-source packages, such as batman \cite{2015PASP..127.1161K} for transit and eclipse modeling, emcee \cite{2013PASP..125..306F} and DYNESTY \cite{2020MNRAS.493.3132S} for MCMC and nested sampling, respectively, and celerite \cite{celerite1, celerite2} and George \cite{2015ITPAM..38..252A} for Gaussian-process (GP) regression. The pipeline is optimized to handle large volumes of time-series data resulting from HST and JWST observations with relative ease, making it suitable for further use in other programs as well.

Upon comparing the lightcurves obtained from both independent reductions, we find that exoTEDRF uncertainties exhibit constant values across large ranges of pixels, with large fluctuations in between. This is particularly noticeable in the binned lightcurves (see Figure \ref{fig:figAerr}) and may have originated from computational approximations at some step of the reduction process. This also results in the exoTEDRF uncertainties appearing slightly underestimated compared to the Eureka! uncertainties. When comparing the white lightcurves, we observe that the fluctuations heavily dominate the exoTEDRF uncertainties (see Figure \ref{fig:figAerr}). In contrast, the Eureka uncertainties appear reasonable in both binned and white lightcurves. This consistency leads us to consider the Eureka! reduced data as more reliable, and we treat it as the default dataset for our primary results.

We implemented a two-stage procedure to fit the lightcurves. In the first stage, we fit the white lightcurve while keeping the mid-transit time and orbital parameters—such as scaled orbital semi-major axis and orbital inclination—free, along with the eclipse depth and detrending parameters (see Table \ref{tab:tr_fit}). In the second stage, we fit the binned spectroscopic lightcurves, further fixing the mid-transit time and orbital parameters based on those estimated from the white lightcurve fit. This approach helps to avoid poor constraints on the wavelength-independent parameters due to lower SNR in the binned lightcurves.

Proper detrending during the lightcurve fitting is crucial to accurately estimate the secondary eclipse depths. We tested various detrending functions, ranging from simple polynomials to GP regression. Since our lightcurves are mostly flat, with trends only over longer time scales, and the secondary eclipse signals have smaller amplitudes compared to the lightcurve SNRs—especially when divided into fine wavelength bins—we found that complex detrending methods like GP regression are not ideal, as they can easily overestimate the eclipse signature as trends. The same issue applies to very complex polynomial functions. After testing several lower-order polynomials, we determined that a fourth-order polynomial detrending best suited our needs, as it provides two symmetric and two asymmetric terms, sufficient for fitting longer time-scale trends observed in our lightcurves. Instead of using a standard fourth-order polynomial, we employed the fourth-order formulation from the Legendre polynomial expansion, which ensures numerical stability when fitting the higher-order terms due to its orthogonality.

Our combined lightcurve fit used a nested sampling algorithm from DYNESTY. The emission spectra obtained from both independent data reductions (see Figure \ref{fig:fig1}) have a mean absolute difference of $\sim$1$\sigma$, indicating excellent statistical agreement between them.

\subsubsection*{Atmospheric retrievals \label{sec:meth4}}

We used petitRADTRANS  (pRT)\cite{2019A&A...627A..67M}, a widely adopted tool in the exoplanet community \cite{2024arXiv241008116K, 2024ApJ...973L..41I, 2024arXiv241203675L, 2024MNRAS.527.7079P, 2024A&A...690A..63B}, to generate synthetic emission spectra for modeling our observed data. We constructed models considering both free and equilibrium chemistry, incorporating several key molecular species such as H$_2$O, CO, CO$_2$, SiO, CH$_4$, H$_2$S, C$_2$H$_2$, HCN, PH$_3$, SO$_2$, TiO, VO, etc. We also included several cloud species expected at these temperatures, such as Fe(s), MgSiO$_3$(s), Al$_2$O$_3$(s), and SiO$_2$(s), keeping their abundances free in both model types. The temperature-pressure profile was based on the prescription of \cite{2010A&A...520A..27G}. For reflected light in the spectra, we considered day-side averaged insolation. Our retrieval framework utilized the pRT retrieval routines \cite{2024JOSS....9.5875N}, which incorporate a nested sampling algorithm using PyMultinest \cite{2009MNRAS.398.1601F, 2014A&A...564A.125B}.

