\section{Related Works}
In this section, we first review the existing literature on the challenges faced by V2X communication and highlight the necessity of GAI-enhanced SemCom in vehicular network. We then summarize the current research on multi-user SemCom, analyzing its limitations and potential improvements. To tackle these challenges, we utilize the proposed SS-MGSC framework to improve multi-user communication performance.

\subsection{Vehicle-to-Everything Communication}
With the ongoing evolution of the Internet of Things and connected devices, vehicular networks have become a key component of modern transportation systems \cite{liu2023bevfusion}. However, they face two key challenges. On the one hand, high computational demands exceed the capabilities of onboard processing units, necessitating edge offloading. On the other hand, the limited field of view of vehicles restricts their ability to acquire comprehensive environmental information, constraining situational awareness and decision-making.

In response to prevailing challenges, V2X communication plays a crucial role in vehicular networks. For instance, vehicles communicating with BS can capture long-range and personalized user demand information, while inter-vehicular communication enhances collaborative perception through shared information. Currently, several studies have explored SemCom within vehicular networks. In our earlier work\cite{lu2024generative}, we proposed a generative multi-modal SemCom framework in IoV, enhancing semantic encoding, SI transmission, and semantic decoding. Similarly, \cite{xu2023semantic} proposed a multi-vehicle collaborative SemCom framework designed for vehicle re-identification. \cite{feng2024semantic} introduced a SemCom-enhanced edge intelligence framework for multi-modal data, but lacks detailed implementation insights.

Although these studies have advanced the field, a single vehicle's perception is often limited by occlusions and distant objects in real-world scenarios. Vehicles relying solely on their onboard sensors may struggle to make accurate decisions, particularly in high-speed or dense traffic conditions. To overcome these limitations, multi-user communication in V2X is essential, enabling vehicles to share SI, compensate for perception blind spots, and collaboratively enhance environmental awareness. By integrating shared SI from multi-users, vehicles can achieve more comprehensive scene understanding, improved decision-making, and greater adaptability to dynamic traffic conditions \cite{liu2024select2col}.

\subsection{Multi-user SemCom}
Current SemCom research primarily focuses on task-specific, point-to-point communication systems, while 6G and future intelligent communication networks demand support for large-scale, multi-user scenarios communication. This challenge is especially pronounced in V2X, where vehicles need to operate in highly dynamic environments with diverse user demands. Relying solely on single-vehicular perception is insufficient due to occlusions and limited sensor range. Addressing these challenges is key to enabling efficient and scalable SemCom for seamless 6G wireless communication.

Although some studies have investigated multi-user SemCom, their progress remains limited and foundational. \cite{xie2022task} introduces a Transformer-based framework to unify transmitter architectures across different tasks, optimizing semantic representation for image retrieval and machine translation while enhancing multi-modal fusion for visual question answering. However, it does not consider reducing redundancy for users with similar or shared semantic needs. To address this, \cite{li2023non} reduces multi-user redundancy by leveraging NOMA technology. By integrating asymmetric quantization and neural network-based detection, it enhances non-orthogonal transmission performance, particularly in low-to-medium SNR scenarios. But as the number of users increases, NOMA faces increasing complexity in successive interference cancellation decoding, posing challenges to scalability.

To tackle redundancy in multi-user content distribution, GAI-enhanced SemCom offers an effective solution. It not only makes the encoding and decoding process more transparent but also establishes a consensus-based semantic knowledge space, improving communication efficiency. Additionally, it enables the creation of a set of semantic representations for different tasks, facilitating the development of a comprehensive semantic knowledge base for multi-user scenarios. A key enabler of such advancements is the DM, which has demonstrated remarkable capabilities in high-fidelity content generation. Unlike traditional generative models, DM iteratively refines a noise distribution through a learned denoising process, achieving state-of-the-art performance in image \cite{saharia2022photorealistic, rombach2022high} and video generation \cite{ho2022imagen}. To enable personalization and task-specific generation, conditional DM incorporates external conditions, such as text prompts for guiding text-to-image generation \cite{zheng2024generative}, segmentation maps for structured scene synthesis \cite{amit2021segdiff}, and edge maps to enhance contour details and refine object structures \cite{xu2024generative}. Among generative foundation models, Stable Diffusion \cite{borji2022generated} stands out for its lightweight 1.7 MB architecture \cite{han2023svdiff}, enabling efficient deployment on resource-constrained vehicular devices for real-time processing. To further enhance generation control, ControlNet \cite{zhang2023adding} extends large pretrained DM by introducing a structured neural network architecture that enables precise conditional guidance, using inputs like sketches, depth maps, and segmentation maps. Extensive experimental results have demonstrated that ControlNet Stable Diffusion significantly outperforms standalone Stable Diffusion in generating more accurate and context-aware outputs \cite{zhao2023uni}.

\subsection{Rate-Splitting Multiple Access}
RSMA is a non-orthogonal multiple access technology that implements rate splitting at the transmitter and successive interference cancellation\cite{zhang2014performance} at the receiver, which divides the transmitted information into a common stream and private streams. At the receiver, the user first decodes the common stream, then removes it and decodes their private streams, while treating the private streams of other users as interference. RSMA effectively addresses the challenges faced by Space Division Multiple Access (SDMA) in managing inter-user interference and reduces the decoding complexity as the number of users increases, which is a limitation of Non-Orthogonal Musltiple Access \cite{mao2018rate,clerckx2019rate}.

Recently, some works have shown the superiority of RSMA for SemCom. \cite{cheng2023resource} addressed the resource allocation problem in SemCom for metaverse construction, utilizing RSMA to transmit differentiated SI to users based on their interest scores. Similarly, \cite{zou2024semantic} aimed to maximize transmission rates for live streaming by leveraging RSMA to partition music as common SI shared among all users and speech as private SI tailored to individual user preferences. \cite{dizdar2024rate} proposed an RSMA-based framework for semantic-aware networks aimed at maximizing the freshness of transmitted information, which demonstrated that RSMA achieves a lower Age of Incorrect Information compared to SDMA. Nevertheless, the aforementioned works primarily focus on SI transmission and are based on evaluation metrics similar to the bit transmission, while neglecting a comprehensive assessment of the overall performance of SemCom.
