\begin{abstract}
  We suggest a protocol for the sympathetic cooling of a molecular asymmetric top rotor co-trapped with laser-cooled atomic ions, 
  based on resonant coupling between the molecular ion's electric dipole moment and a common vibrational mode of the trapped particles. 
  By combining sympathetic sideband laser cooling with coherent microwave excitation, we demonstrate the efficient depopulation of arbitrary rotational subspaces and the ability to cool an incoherent distribution of rotational states into a single, well-defined quantum state. This capability opens the door to exploiting the rotational Hilbert space for applications in quantum information processing and high-precision spectroscopy.
 \end{abstract}


\maketitle

Polyatomic molecules are emerging as a new platform for fundamental physics \cite{SafronovaRMP18,HutzlerQST20} and quantum information processing \cite{Albert_PRL_2020}. They are, for example, excellent candidates to search for a non-zero dipole moment of the electron~\cite{AndereggScience2023} or to measure parity violation with chiral molecules~\cite{QuackChemSci2022,ErezPRX23}. Molecular rotation can be used for quantum error correction~\cite{AlbertPRX20,Shubham_2023,FureyQuantum24} or quantum simulation of magnetism~\cite{WallAnnPhys2013}. All these applications take advantage of the fact that the Hilbert space of molecular rotation is large and comparatively well isolated. Moreover, the rotational spectrum is anharmonic which eases quantum state control~\cite{KochRMP19}, and, within finite subspaces, both linear rotors and asymmetric top rotors are completely controllable~\cite{Judson1990, Pozzoli21, Leibscher22}.
This implies that, with a combination of resonant electric fields, typically in the microwave, any desired unitary operation on the subspace can be implemented, degeneracies notwithstanding~\cite{Leibscher22}.
However, to actually implement the desired level of control requires the trapping and cooling of molecules~\cite{Deiss2023,Sin22b} which for polyatomic molecules is very challenging. To date, the most advanced method is direct laser cooling~\cite{AugenbraunAdvAMO,VilasNature2024,LasnerPRL25}. It is applicable to a certain class of molecules that was derived based on chemical intuition in order to adapt a spectrum with closed cycling transitions from diatomic to polyatomic molecules~\cite{IsaevPRL2016}.

Here, we suggest a completely different approach to cooling the rotations in polyatomic molecules, applicable to molecular ions, but not requiring a specific electronic structure. 
We suggest to leverage  resonant dipole-phonon interaction, as discussed for diatomic ions~\cite{HudsonPRA2018,Campbell_PRL_2020}, to sympathetically cool the rotations of polyatomic molecular ions by laser-cooled atomic ions. 
In diatomic molecules, the normal mode frequencies in ion trap---typically in the MHz range---can be tuned to match hyperfine transitions \cite{Campbell_PRL_2020}, while dipole-allowed rotational transitions typically lie in the GHz to THz regime, and are therefore far off-resonant with trap modes. In contrast, polyatomic molecules that are asymmetric top rotors exhibit a much richer rotational spectrum, which can include dipole-allowed transitions within the MHz range. This enables resonant coupling between molecular rotation and trap motion via the permanent dipole moment of the molecule.
We show that resonant dipole-phonon coupling allows for sympathetic sideband cooling of the coupled rotational states, and propose a combination of sideband cooling with coherent microwave excitation to cool or deplete an arbitrary rotational subspace of an asymmetric top molecule. 

