We consider a polyatomic molecular ion co-trapped in a linear Paul trap with two atomic ions as depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig:trap}(a). 
The harmonic trap potential couples the center of mass motion of the trapped particles --- their collective vibration is described by normal modes with frequencies $\omega_p$. The motion of the atomic and molecular ions are coupled, if both oscillate with non-vanishing amplitude about their equilibrium position. This is true, in the case of the three-particle chain in Fig.~\ref{fig:trap}, for 6 out of all the 9 modes.  %chr: bitte noch eintragen
If the molecules are polar, the dipole interaction $H_{dp}=-{\bm \mu}  \cdot {\bf R}\cdot {\bf E}$, where ${\bf R}$ is the rotation matrix that describes the orientation of the molecule fixed dipole moment ${\bm \mu}$ and the space fixed electric field ${\bf E}$, couples the rotational degrees of freedom of the molecule to the collective center of mass motion of the trapped particles~\cite{HudsonPRA2018,Campbell_PRL_2020,Leibscher_Kahler_Koch_arxiv}.
We denote the eigenstates of the trapped particles by $|a,n_p,j\rangle = \ket{a} \otimes \ket{n_p} \otimes \ket{\phi_j}$ where $a=g,e$ denote the internal states of the atoms, $n_p=0,1,2,...$ the vibrational state of the trapped particles in normal mode $p$. 
If one of the normal mode frequencies $\omega_p$ is resonant to a dipole allowed rotational transition between the rotational states $\ket{\phi_{j_1}}$ and $\ket{\phi_{j_2}}$ with $E_{j_2} > E_{j_1}$ strong resonant dipole interaction couples the states $\ket{a,n_p,j_1}$ and $\ket{a,n_p-1,j_2}$, while dipole coupling with all other rotational states is negligible.
In this case,  the degenerate states mix to 
\begin{eqnarray}
     |a,n_p,j_\pm \rangle = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \left( |a,n_p,j_1 \rangle \pm  |a,n_p-1,j_2 \rangle \right)
\end{eqnarray}
and the degeneracy is lifted by the dipole splitting
\begin{equation}
 \Delta E_{n_p} = |\bra{n_p,j_1} H_{dp} \ket{n_p-1,j_2}|,
 \label{eq:dipole_splitting}
\end{equation}
see Fig.~\ref{fig:trap} (b).
%results, in second order perturbation, in an energy splitting
%\begin{equation}
%  \Delta E^{(p)}_{n_p,j} = \frac{|\bra{n_p-1,j_2} H_{dp}^{(p)} \ket{n_p,j_1}|^2}{E_{n_p,j_2} - E_{n_p-1,j_1}}.
%\end{equation}
%The normal modes in an ion trap are typically in the order of MHz. Resonant dipole interaction can thus occur between hyperfine states of diatomic molecules \cite{Campell_PRL_2020}.
%Polyatomic asymmetric top molecules have a complex rotational spectrum with dipole allowed rotational transitions ranging from MHz to GHz. 
The coupling between the states $\ket{n_p,j_1}$ and 
$\ket{n_p-1,j_2}$ for $n_p > 0$ makes the rotational states
$\ket{\phi_{j_1}}$ and $\ket{\phi_{j_2}}$ amenable for sympathetic sideband cooling: Due to the coupling, laser cooling does not only cool the center of mass motion of the molecule to the vibrational ground state but also 
causes a decay of the rotational states population from level $j_2$ to level $j_1$. Dipole coupling thus creates a decay channel from the rotational level $j_2$ to $j_1$, allowing to deplete the rotational level $j_2$. This decay channel relies on the resonant coupling between two particular rotational states. While all other rotational states are not sufficiently affected by the interaction to be cooled, their population can be changed with microwave pulses.
%If the dipole moment of anasymmetric top molecule has  at least two non-vanishing components, its rotational subspace is controllable by a combination of $x-$, $y-$, and $z-$polarized microwave pulses \cite{Leibscher22}.
We propose to combine the high degree of control that microwave pulses have on the rotational dynamics with dipole induced sympathetic cooling of the rotational states in order to deplete a rotational subspace. 

%making polyatomic molecules particular amenable for dipole induced quantum logic spectroscopy and sympathetic cooling. We propose protocols for dipole induced sympathetic sideband cooling in combination with coherent manipulation of rotational state population by microwave excitation in order to deplete a rotational subspace and in such way make it accessible for quantum computing of high precession spectroscopy and to cool a rotational manifold to a single rotational state. To this end, we consider a polyatomic molecular ion co-trapped with two atomic ions in a linear Paul trap \cite{Leibscher_Kahler_Koch_draft} as depticted in Fig.~\ref{fig:trap}~(a).
