As an example, we consider two Yb$^+$ ions with mass $M_a = 173$ u cotrapped with a glutamine ion with mass $M_{rot} = 147$ u, cf. Fig.~\ref{fig:trap}. Atomic and molecular ions are chosen such that they have comparable masses. This ensures an effective coupling between the center of mass motions of all trapped particles \cite{Leibscher_Kahler_Koch_arxiv} which is essential for sympathetic sideband cooling. 
As a chiral molecule, glutamine has a dipole moment with three non-vanishing Cartesian components in the molecular frame, allowing full control of its rotational dynamics by microwave fields \cite{Pozzoli21,Leibscher22}. Moreover, it is a near prolate asymmetric top, with
eigenstates  $\ket{\phi_j} = \ket{J_{K_a,K_b},M}$, with the rotational quantum number $J=0,1,2,...$, the magnetic quantum number $M=-J,-J+1,...,J$ and $K_a,K_c = 0,1,...J$. 
%Note that the energy eigenvalues $E_j^{rot} = E_{J_{K_a,K_c}}^{rot}$ do not depend on $M$, so that each rotational state is $2J+1$-fold degenerate, as it is depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig:trap} (c). 
The spectrum of a near prolate asymmetric top contains closely lying pairs of rotational states $\ket{J_{K_a,K_b},M}$, $\ket{J_{K_a,K_b-1},M}$ with transition frequencies of the order of a few MHz.  The dipole interaction is particularly strong if the trap frequencies are adjusted such that the radial center of mass mode, sketched in Fig.~\ref{fig:trap} (a), is resonant to the rotational transition between the states $\ket{3_{2,2},M}$ and $\ket{3_{2,1},M}$, i.e. $\omega_p = E_{3_{2,1}}-E_{3_{2,2}} = 16.85$ MHz. Note that each rotational state is $2J+1$-fold degenerate, as depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig:trap} (c), and the dipole induced energy splitting,
\begin{equation}
    \Delta E_{n_p} = \sqrt{n_p} \left |{\cal E}^{(p)}_0 \mu_a 
    \bra{3_{22},M} R_{za} \ket{3_{21},M} \right |\,,
\end{equation}
depends on the orientational quantum number $M$ {\it via} the rotational transition matrix elements $\bra{3_{22},M} R_{za} \ket{3_{21},M}$ \cite{Leibscher22b}. For $M=0$, transitions between rotational states with same $J$ are forbidden, i.e., there is no energy splitting between the states $\ket{3_{2,2},0}$ and $\ket{3_{2,1},0}$. For $M\neq0$, the dipole-splitting is 
