We simulate the cooling of an ensemble of rotational states by
numerically solving the Lindblad equation
\begin{eqnarray}
    \frac{\partial \rho}{\partial t} = - \frac{i}{\hbar} \left[ H, \rho \right] +
    {\cal L}_D \rho
    \label{eq:Lindblad}
\end{eqnarray}
with the dissipator ${\cal L}_D \rho = L \rho L^\dagger - \frac{1}{2} \left[ L^\dagger L, \rho \right]$
describing the dissipative channels sketched in Fig.~\ref{fig:trap}(b). 
%The Hamiltonian $H=H_0+H_{int}(t)$ describes the coherent dynamics of the trapped particles. 
In order to demonstrate that a dipole splitting of the order several kHz is sufficient for sympathetic cooling, we consider a subset of the asymmetric top states, as depicted by the dark gray lines in Fig.~\ref{fig:trap}(c). The dipole coupling between the states $\ket{3_{2,2},M}$ and $\ket{3_{2,1},M}$ is indicated by wiggled gray arrows. Note that the states $\ket{2_{1,1},M}$ are not affected by dipole coupling. We assume that initially, all states of this rotational subsystem are populated, as depicted in panel (i) of Fig.~\ref{fig:sideband_cooling} (a). The target is to deplete all rotational states except $\ket{3_{2,2},M}$, see panel (vi), or to even cool the rotational ensemble of states to a single quantum state, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:cooling_degenerate}.
%The dipole interaction couples states with $\Delta M =0$ so that the Lindblad equation can be numerically solved separately for every value of $M$. 
%To model the cooling process, we consider the subsystem depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig:trap} (b), details are enclosed as supplemental material. 

Figure \ref{fig:sideband_cooling}(b) and (c) demonstrate the decay of the population from rotational states $3_{21}$ within 5 ms while the atomic ions are irradiated by a laser pulse; all vibrational states decay to $n_p=0$ and the  rotational states $3_{21}, M \neq0$ decay to 
$3_{22}, M$, with faster decay for states with large $|M|$. Since there is no dipole coupling for $M=0$, the state $3_{21}, M$ is not affected by the cooling mechanism. The final population of the rotational states, depicted in panel (ii) of Fig.~\ref{fig:sideband_cooling} (a), shows that except for $M=0$, the population of the higher lying rotational state can be depleted via sympathetic sideband cooling. 

In order to further cool the rotational degrees of freedom the molecule, i.e., to deplete additional rotational states, a combination of sideband cooling and microwave excitation can be utilized. By coherently transferring population to the coupled rotational states and then cool these states, one can successively deplete more and more rotational states. 
%In addition, we mark another arbitrary rotational level, $2_{21}$. With microwave pulses with frequency $\omega_0 = E_{3_{21}} - E_{2_{21}}/\hbar$ one can coherently drive transitions between the states $\ket{2_{21},M}$ and $\ket{3_{21},M'}$.
The blue arrows in Fig.~\ref{fig:trap}(c) mark coherent transitions induced by microwave pulses which can be described by the Hamiltonian
\begin{equation}
H_{MW} = -\bm{\mu} \cdot {\bm R}\cdot \bm{E} (t)\,,
\label{eq:H_MW}
\end{equation}
where 
$\bm{E}(t) = \bm{e} {\cal E} (t) \cos(\omega t)$
is the electric field with polarization $\bm{e} =\bm{e}_x$, $\bm{e}_y$ or $\bm{e}_z$, amplitude ${\cal E} (t)$ and frequency $\omega$.  Microwave pulses polarized in $z$-direction induce transitions between states with $\Delta M=0$ (see dashed blue arrows in Fig.~\ref{fig:trap}(c)), $x$- and $y$-polarized fields drive transitions with $\Delta M = \pm 1$ (solid blue arrows). Furthermore, dipole selection rules allow transitions between asymmetric top states with $\Delta J = 0, \pm 1$. The frequency of the microwave is set to $\omega = (E_{3_{21}} - E_{2_{11}})/\hbar$ so that it resonantly drives transitions between the states $\ket{2_{11},M}$ and $\ket{3_{21},M'}$. 
The target is to completely deplete the levels $2_{11}$ and $3_{21}$ so that at the end of the pulse sequence, only one rotational level, namely $3_{22}$ is populated. 
To this end, we apply an $x$-polarized microwave pulse
allowing transitions from and to the $M=0$ states. Alternating coherent microwave excitation with cooling steps thus allows not only to deplete the $M=0$ state, but also to empty the population of any additional rotational level, here the level $2_{11}$, see panels (iii) - (vi) of Fig.~\ref{fig:sideband_cooling} (a). 
%and thus to further cool the rotational state distribution.
As seen in Fig.~\ref{fig:sideband_cooling} (d), the target is reached with very good accuracy after only few iterations if the microwave pulse is perfectly x-polarized. 

In an experiment, the microwave pulses typically have a slight ellipticity instead of being perfectly linearly polarized, which we simulate by adding a $z$-polarized component to the pulse. Figure ~\ref{fig:sideband_cooling}(d) shows that with imperfect polarization, the process needs more iterations to achieve comparably good depletion, but is in principle robust to impurities in the polarization of the pulses. Only with  a purely z-polarized pulse (dotted line), the target population cannot be obtained. The reason is, that without a $x$- (or $y$-) component in the polarizability, there are no transitions with $\Delta  M = \pm 1$, i.e., the population is stuck in the $3_{21}$, $M=0$ state. 

Since a quantum asymmetric top rotors is always controllable with a sequence of $x$, $y$ and $z$-polarized fields if its dipole moment has at least two non-vanishing components \cite{Leibscher22}, a similar control sequence can be used to deplete any other rotational level. If there is no dipole allowed transition between that level and level $3_{21}$, e.g. because $|\Delta J| > 1$, the population of the desired level must be first coherently transferred to an already empty dipole allowed level before the cooling cycle described above can be applied. 
%It should be noted that the depleting scheme relies on controllability of a quantum asymmetric top, i.e. requires at least two non-zero components of the molecular dipole moment, which occurs in chiral molecules.
Thus, an arbitrary subspace of rotational states can be depleted. 

\begin{figure}[tbp]
	%\includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{cooling_MW_scheme_degenerate.jpg}
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figure3.jpg}
	\caption{(a) Microwave excitation and cooling scheme for cooling of initially degenerate rotational states to the single state $\ket{3_{22},3}$, marked by the yellow box. The decay from the rotational states $\ket{3_{21},M}$ to $\ket{3_{22},M}$ is indicated by wiggled gray arrows,  blue and orange arrows indicate the transitions driven by microwave pulses  (b) Fidelity of the target state population as function of number of iterations for microwave pulses with perfect $\sigma_+$-polarization (solid line) as well as with pulses that have in addition $1$ \% (dashed line) , $5$ \% (dash-dotted line), and $10$ \%  (dotted line) $\sigma_-$polarization.}
	\label{fig:cooling_degenerate}
\end{figure}
In principle, this protocol allows for cooling arbitrary distributions of rotational states to the degenerate rotational manifold $3_{21},M$. However, it does not allow cooling to a single rotational quantum state. 
Cooling a degenerate ensemble of rotational states to a single rotational state requires a protocol with at least two different microwave frequencies for coherent population transfer, as depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig:cooling_degenerate}(a)
where we start with an incoherent ensemble of degenerate rotational states in level $3_{22}$.
The target state is a single rotational state, namely $\ket{3_{22},3}$.
as marked in yellow in Fig.~\ref{fig:cooling_degenerate}(a). 
The protocol starts with a circularly polarized microwave pulse with frequency $\omega = (E_{3_{21}} - E_{3_{22}})/\hbar$ exciting $\Delta M = + 1$ transitions from level $3_{22}$ to level $3_{21}$, see orange arrows in Fig.~\ref{fig:cooling_degenerate}(a).
The combination of coherently driving $\Delta M = +1$ transitions and subsequent cooling along a $\Delta M = 0$ transition results in a shift of the population toward the rotational state with largest value of $M$, i.e. $M=3$. However, since there is no decay channel for $M=0$, part of the population is stuck in state $\ket{3_{21},0}$. This can be amended by depleting the population in $\ket{3_{21},0}$ with a $z$-polarized pulse with frequency resonant to the $3_{21} \leftrightarrow 2_{11}$ transition (dashed blue line in Fig.~\ref{fig:cooling_degenerate}(b)) and subsequently driving the population to state  $\ket{3_{21},1}$ by a right circularly polarized pulse with the same frequency. The population of $\ket{3_{21},1}$ can then decay to $\ket{3_{22},1}$ by a cooling cycle and process (a) can be repeated. The complete microwave and cooling sequence can then be repeated several times until only the target state is populated. The fidelity of this process is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:cooling_degenerate}(c), black line. With an increasing number of iterations, the target state can be attained with arbitrarily good fidelity for perfectly polarized pulses.
When allowing for non-perfect polarization of the microwave pulses, the final fidelity is sensitive to the imperfection, cf. Fig.~\ref{fig:cooling_degenerate}(c) showing the fidelity for excitation with microwave pulses which are not perfectly right circularly polarized but  also have contributions of left polarization ($1 - 10\%$). 
%The fidelity is limited depending on the amount of the undesired polarization. 
The error is due to degenerate states being distinguished only by polarization and not by frequency selection which is also why the cooling transition must be of the same frequency as the driving transition. A clear circular polarization of the microwaves~\cite{Yuan_RevSciInstr_2023} is thus essential for the realization of this cooling scheme. 

Our protocol for cooling the rotational degrees of freedom is easily adapted to molecular ions other than the glutamine ion. For glutamine, the protocol relied on the closely spaced pairs of rotational levels of a near prolate asymmetric top, but similar level spacings can also be found in near oblate asymmetric tops. Furthermore, in order to be amenable to sympathetic sideband cooling, the molecular ions have to be polar and of $C_1$ or $C_s$ symmetry, to ensure sufficiently many non-vanishing Cartesian components of the dipole moment and thus controllability within every rotational subspace. The molecular mass should be comparable to the mass of the co-trapped atomic ions such that the center of mass motion of atomic and molecular ions is sufficiently coupled. 
Finally, it may also be possible to extend the protocol to diatomic molecular ions. While their rotational level spacings are far too large for resonant dipole coupling~\cite{Leibscher_Kahler_Koch_arxiv}, the cooling protocol could be leverage transitions between hyperfine states.

In conclusion, we have shown how resonant dipole-phonon coupling enables sympathetic sideband cooling of the rotations of polyatomic molecules. Combining  sideband cooling and coherent microwave excitation, an arbitrary subspace of the rotational spectrum of an asymmetric top can be depleted, transferring all population into a single pure rotational state. The cooling scheme requires strong resonant dipole-coupling between the rotational degrees of freedom and the collective motion of the trapped particles. A large molecular dipole moment and closely lying rotational levels are thus favorable. 
The complex rotational structure of asymmetric top molecules 
provides dipole allowed rotational transitions that allow for such resonant dipole coupling --- the cooling scheme is thus is particularly well suited for polyatomic molecules.  
Our cooling protocol enables the preparation of polyatomic molecular ions in a single quantum state. It thus opens the door to extending quantum logic spectroscopy from diatomic~\cite{WolfNature_2016,SinhalScience2020} to polyatomic molecules and to using the rich rotational spectrum of asymmetric top rotors in fundamental physics experiments or quantum simulation and error correction. 

