The matrix $\M{D}$ will likely be familiar, as this is the matrix used by the well known Block Jacobi preconditioner \cite{lawn21}.
For a matrix with a reasonably small band width the matrix $\M{D}$ should contain the majority of the elements.
The matrix $\M{D}$ may be solved with perfect parallelism\textemdash there isn't any coupling between adjacent submatrices $\M{A_i}$, so each partition may be associated with a compute element and operations can be performed locally. 
$\M{S}$, on the other hand, could be viewed as representing necessary communication between neighboring partitions (in the next section, the use of low-rank approximations inside $\M{S}$ will be investigated, and the result is a preconditioner which bridges the gap between conventional \spike{} based solvers and the Block Jacobi preconditioner).  

