\noindent \textbf{Variable selection}, 
also known as feature selection,  
is a critical process to choose the most relevant variables representing the target outcome of interest, enhancing both model performance and interpretation. 
%By identifying and utilizing only the most significant variables, variable selection not only enhances the accuracy of predictions by avoiding overfitting but also makes models more interpretable and easier to understand, especially with high-dimensional data. 
Over the past few decades, many well-known methods have been established, ranging from classical LASSO, Fisher score, and kernel dimension reduction \citep{tibshirani1996regression,gu2012generalized,chen2017kernel,zhou2021parsimonious}, towards deep learning \citep{liang2018bayesian,balin2019concrete,lee2021self,cai2023learning,zhang2023towards}. Yet, these works either \textit{suffer from model-based constraints or lack theoretical guarantees}. 
\textbf{The model-X knockoff method}  proposed by \citet{candes2018panning} aims to achieve both goals via a general variable selection framework for black-box algorithms with guarantees of false discovery rate control.  
Due to its model-agnostic nature, the knockoff method has been extended to complement a wide range of variable selection approaches \citep{sesia2017gene,ma2021powerful,liu2022generalized}. The main price or central challenge within the knockoff method lies in \textit{the generation of faithful knockoff features}. Existing techniques either use model-specific methods \citep[see e.g., ][]{sesia2017gene,liu2018auto} that assume the underlying covariate distribution, or model-free approaches \citep[see e.g., ][]{jordon2018Knockoffgan,romano2020deep} that utilize deep generative models to obtain knockoffs without further assumptions on feature distribution.  
Owing to the blessing of online RL, our method bypasses this challenge through the known joint distribution of actions represented by the ongoing policy network, and thus can easily resample the action values to create exact knockoff features. 



% or flexible. Model-specific methods, such as those based on Gaussian, hidden Markov models \citep{sesia2017gene}, or AutoEncoding knockoffs \citep{liu2018auto}, rely on assumptions about the underlying covariate distribution, which can be problematic when the data does not conform to these assumptions. Alternatively, flexible approaches, like KnockoffGAN \citep{jordon2018Knockoffgan} and Deep knockoffs \citep{romano2020deep}, utilize deep generative models to generate knockoffs without necessitating any assumptions about the feature distribution.  Beyond these existing works, under online RL, our proposed method can bypass this challenge through the known joint distribution of actions represented by the ongoing policy network, and thus we can easily resample the action values to create exact knockoff features. 
