Gradient descent is an optimization algorithm that leverages gradient information to iteratively update parameters. 
Given the computational intensity and statistical uncertainties inherent in QMC calculations, complex optimization schemes are infeasible.
We adopt a stable and efficient gradient descent method to perform the optimization task, summarized as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Initialization}: Select a reasonable initial parameter set $\theta_0$.

    \item \textbf{Parameter rescaling}: Rescale parameters to ensure comparable variation scales. 
    During early iterations:
    $$
    \theta_i \rightarrow \theta_i\left| \frac{\partial \chi^2}{\partial \theta_i} \right|,
    $$
    yielding partial derivatives of magnitude $\sim 1$. 
    Near convergence when iterations slow, apply fixed scaling:
    $$
    \theta_i \rightarrow \theta_i \times k_i,
    $$
    with scaling factors: $
    k_{s_L} = 1.853 \times 10^5,  k_{C_2} = 3.946 \times 10^{11}, k_{C_3} = 1.004 \times 10^{18},$ and $ k_{C_{s}} = 1.260 \times 10^{15}.$
    This technique significantly accelerates convergence.

    \item \textbf{Gradient computation}: At iteration $t$, compute partial derivatives of the loss function. 
    For $C_2$, $C_3$, and $C_{s}$, directly measure energy contributions from corresponding terms. 
    For $s_{\rm L}$ (nonlinear in binding energy), apply the Feynman-Hellmann theorem for derivative estimation, achieving Monte Carlo uncertainties $< 0.1\%$.

    \item \textbf{Parameter update}: 
    $$
    \theta_{t+1} = \theta_t - \alpha \frac{\partial \chi^2(\theta_t)}{\partial \theta},
    $$
    with adaptive learning rate:
    $$
    \alpha =
    \begin{cases} 
    \alpha \times 2 & \text{if } \chi^2 \text{ decreases} \\ 
    \alpha \times 0.2 & \text{otherwise}
    \end{cases}
.
    $$     This strategy increases step size in smooth regions and decreases it to prevent instability or overshooting.          \item \textbf{Convergence check}: Terminate when:     $$
    |\chi^2(\theta_{t+1}) - \chi^2(\theta_t)| < \epsilon.
    $$
\end{enumerate}

