\section{Conclusions}
\label{Sec:Conclusions}

We have presented a generalised formulation to handle coupled phase field fracture and corrosion problems under the same theoretical structure. The versatility of the heat transfer equation is then exploited to provide a novel computational framework capable of handling coupled phase field problems. This significantly facilitates the numerical implementation, which can be done at the integration point level in commercial finite element packages. This is demonstrated in Abaqus but the approach is universal and could be adopted in other platforms. The applications explored span hydraulic fracture, thermal fracture, hydrogen embrittlement and stress-assisted corrosion. 2D and 3D problems are considered. The associated user material subroutines are made freely available to showcase the simplicity of the approach. The results obtained with the novel computational framework presented are well-aligned with experimental and computational results from the literature involving the quenching of ceramic plates, the propagation of pressure and fluid injection-driven cracks in porous media, the growth of cracks assisted by hydrogen in metallic materials, and the corrosion of metals and their interplay with mechanical fields. 

