\section{Introduction}


It is 1666 and England is facing the Great Plague in London. In that year, Cambridge University was closed because of the epidemic, and Newton, who was then a young student, retired to his home at Woolsthorpe Manor. It is a spring morning and young Newton is enjoying the light breeze and early sunshine beneath an apple tree.
Immersed in thought, between infinitesimal calculations and prisms an apple fell on his head.
\textsl{Eureka!} -- he exclaimed.
\textsl{The apple would never have fallen if a force had not caused it to do so, the same force that keeps the Earth in orbit around the Sun}, Newton thought.
\textsl{And that's not all} --- he continued --- \textsl{the natural tendency of a body to resist changes in its state of motion is why the free surface of water within a bucket rotating with respect to absolute space takes on a concave shape.}
Suddenly, the qualitative ideas of Galileo and Descartes crystallised in a rigorous form in the mind of Newton, who conceived his three laws of dynamics, including the first: the law (or principle) of inertia.

This, of course, is just a tale.

Newton stands in continuity with a long tradition of thinkers in the West, known as \textit{natural philosophers}, who questioned Nature and its laws.
Among these questions is that concerning the motion of bodies.

The motion of bodies is a topic over 2,000 years old.
\textit{How} bodies move in nature?
This question is accompanied by another, perhaps more profound question: \textit{why} do bodies move in the way they do? What is the nature of their motion?

These questions, when they arose, were often posed in terms of a division: There are motions caused by agents external to the bodies and motions intrinsic to the bodies themselves when no external cause acts.
So the questions become: \textit{how} do bodies move when no external cause acts on them? And how do they move when there is such a cause?
And \textit{why} do they assume such and such a configuration of motion?

The first investigator of inertia was Aristotle with his distinction between ‘\textit{natural motions}’, typical of bodies ‘left to themselves’, and ‘\textit{violent motions}’, typical when an external influence is present \citep{sachs1995aristotle}.

In the late Middle Ages, Buridan advanced the \textit{impetus theory}, positing that a body in motion carries an internal ‘impetus’ that leads it to continue its motion even in the absence of external causes \citep{Jung2011}. This is historically the first definition of inertia similar to the one that would later be refined in modern times by Galileo and Descartes and then rigorously formalised by Newton in his ‘first law’.\footnote{For an analysis on the origin and role of the Law of Inertia in Newton's thinking see \cite{Earman1973}.}

Finally, in the twentieth century, Einstein placed himself in continuity with his modern predecessors (Galileo and Newton in particular) and proposed the \textit{geodesic principle}, analogous to Newton's first law, in order to answer millennia-old doubts about what is inertia: free bodies follow geodesics of a curved spacetime.
Moreover, general relativity (GR) seemed to elegantly answer not only the how, but also the \textit{why} of the motion of free bodies \citep{Weatherall2011,Weatherall2016-WEAIME}.\footnote{The recent literature on whether and in what sense GR has the power to \textit{explain} inertial motion originates with \cite{Brown2005-kq}. Brown does not refer to a specific account of explanation, he uses the term in the straightforward liberal meaning that is: giving \textit{sufficient} conditions for something; or giving some \textit{insights} into that something; or also the possibility to \textit{formally derive} that something. See \cite{sep-scientific-explanation} for an overview in scientific explanation. \cite{Weatherall2016-WEAIME} advocates the so-called \textit{Puzzle-ball conjecture}, which is compatible with an account of explanation which is termed not in terms of derivability from more fundamental principles, but only in terms of \textit{interconnectedness}. In such terms, the explanation is not asymmetrical: one does not have a set of principles explaining another, but a kind of \textit{relative explanation between principles}. \lq{}\lq{}[G]eneral relativity explains inertial [i.e. geodesic] motion by appeal to Einstein’s equation, but it may equally well explain Einstein’s equation by appeal to the geodesic principle\rq{}\rq{} (ivi, p.38).}


In this paper, drawing on the work of Galileo, Newton and Einstein, I aim to shed light on what inertia is, with a particular focus on the concept of inertial motion in GR.
My analysis will reveal that inertia is a useful fantasy and that the natural motion of bodies follows other laws.


\section{Inertial Motion and the Principle of Inertia: The Classical Story}\label{sec1}

The concept of inertial motion has long been a cornerstone of classical mechanics and modern physics in general. The most common definition, which dates back to Galileo, posits:\footnote{Galileo never penned a “definition” of inertia in the modern textbook sense. Instead, through his experiments—most notably in his work \textit{Two New Sciences} (\citeyear{galilei1914dialogues})—he argued that if all friction and resistance were removed, a body in motion would continue moving at a constant speed in a straight line. His thought experiments and observations (such as those using inclined planes) laid the groundwork for what we now call the principle of inertia, first formalised by Newton in his \textit{Principia} (\citeyear{newton1687principia}).}

\begin{definition}
    \textbf{Inertial Motion. Version 1}: The tendency of objects in uniform motion to continue their uniform motion and objects at rest to remain at rest. \label{INERTIA1}
\end{definition}

Building upon this, the Principle of Inertia (\textbf{PIN}), aiming to answer the question of \textit{why} bodies move as they do has been formulated in two distinct ways:
\begin{definition}
    \textbf{PIN. v.1} Bodies maintain inertial motion if no \textit{force} acts upon them. \label{PIN1}
    \end{definition}
    \begin{definition}
    \textbf{PIN. v.2} Bodies \textit{sufficiently} distant from other objects retain their state of inertial motion. \cite{einstein2015relativity}. \label{PIN2}
\end{definition}

Both versions, however, are encumbered by conceptual difficulties which undermine the foundational clarity of the concept of inertia.


The term \lq{}\textit{force}\rq{} in Definition \eqref{PIN1} hinges upon the deviation from inertial motion,  since a force is defined as the cause of such deviation.
This introduces a circularity: \lq{}\lq{}\textit{Bodies maintain inertial motion if they do not deviate from inertial motion}\rq{}\rq{}.
Similarly, Definition \eqref{PIN2} relies on the notion of ‘sufficient distance’, which itself presupposes inertial motion
In fact, the notion of \lq{}sufficiency\rq{} is defined as \lq{}enough to move inertially\rq{}, so that Definition \eqref{PIN2}, would read as: \lq{}\lq{}\textit{Bodies which move inertially, retain their state of inertial motion.}\rq{}\rq{}



A natural attempt to refine these definitions is to incorporate explicitly the role of \textit{inertial reference frames}.
This requires first defining a reference frame and then an \textit{inertial} reference frame (\textbf{INRF}).
In an operational spirit, following \cite{Bamonti2023}, a \textit{reference frame} may be defined as a set of four degrees of freedom $\{x^I\}\mid_{I=1,\dots,4}$ \textit{instantiated} by a concrete physical system (three rods and one clock), which provides a (local) diffeomorphism $U\subseteq \mathcal{M} \to \mathbb{R}^4$ and which \textit{uniquely} assigns four numbers to each point $p \in U$. This general definition extends readily to relativistic spacetime theories such as GR.
In theories with fixed background spacetime, like special relativistic theories, the reference frame becomes a \textit{global} map preserving the spatiotemporal structure:
\begin{equation}
x^I:(M,\eta_{ab})\to\mathbb{R}^4,
\end{equation}
where $\{x^I\}$ defines a \textit{Poincaré} map. Analogously, in Newtonian theory (in covariant form), one obtains a Galilean map\footnote{Here $t_{ab}$ represents the temporal metric structure with signature $(1,0,0,0)$ and $h^{ab}$ the spatial metric structure with signature $(0,1,1,1)$; $\xrightarrow{\sim}$ denotes an isomorphic mapping between spaces. The connection $\nabla$ is flat (i.e.\ $R^{a}_{\;bcd}=0$) and is one of the infinite compatible flat connections satisfying $\nabla_a t_{bc}=0$ and $\nabla_a h_{bc}=0$.} 
\begin{equation}
x^I:(M,t_{ab},h^{ab},\nabla)\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^3\to\mathbb{R}^4.
\end{equation}

With this in hand, we can define an inertial reference frame as follows:\footnote{It is important to clarify that an \textbf{INRF} $\{x^{I}\}$ could also be understood to be ‘attached’ to the body whose inertial motion is attributed. This will be useful in \S\ref{geodesic} where inertial frames will be analysed in the case of GR and will turn out to be \textit{only locally defined} and \textit{comoving} with the body.}
\begin{definition}
    \textbf{INRF. v.1}: An inertial reference frame provides a standard of measurement for space and time such that:
\begin{itemize}
\item Bodies not subject to \textit{net external forces} move uniformly $\bigl( \frac{dx^{i=1,2,3}}{dt}\bigr)=\text{const.}$, with $t\equiv x^4$.\footnote{Of course, in the Newtonian framework the temporal parameter is the same for all frames: $x^4\equiv t$, given the global nature of time.} 
\item The accelerated motion of bodies of mass $m$ is proportional to and in the direction of applied forces $\bigl(F=m\frac{d^2x^i}{dt^2}\bigr)$.
\item No fictitious forces (e.g., centrifugal, Coriolis) are present.\footnote{This type of definition is often referred to as a \textit{law-based definition}. For example, this is used in \cite{sep-spacetime-iframes} where an inertial frame is defined as a spatial reference frame together with some means of measuring time, so that uniform motions can be distinguished from accelerated motions.  Thus, an observer embodying inertial reference frame sees \textit{non-inertial} bodies that respect $F=ma$.  \lq{}\lq{}[\dots] an inertial frame is a reference-frame with a time-scale, relative to which the motion of a body not subject to forces is always rectilinear and uniform, accelerations are always proportional to and in the direction of applied forces, and applied forces are always met with equal and opposite reactions [\dots] in accord with Newton’s laws of motion. (ivi, p.1)\rq{}\rq{} However, there is also another type of definition, called \textit{structure-based} (see \citealp[\S4]{JacobsForthcoming-JACHNT-2}). Instead of using dynamical justifications in terms of forces, these definitions claim that every spacetime has a preferred set of frames that reflects the structure inherent in the spacetime, such as the affine structure \citep{Earman1973,Earman1992-jn} (see also \citealp[\S2]{Bamonti2023}). In more detail, procedure of a structure-based definition is to (i) stipulate some spatiotemporal structure, (ii) claim that certain frames best represent this structure, and (iii) restrict the \textit{inertial} frames to just those ones. See \S\ref{lawvsprin} for further discussions on this point.}
\end{itemize} \label{INRF}
\end{definition}

%However, it is also possible to define the inertial motion of an object with respect to a different given \textbf{INRF} $\{y^{I}\}$ which share the same global temporal structure. In such a case, the object will follow a law of motion without forces $\frac{d^2y^i}{dt}=0$. Of course, it is also possible the case where the object moves non-inertially and follows $F=m\frac{d^2y^i}{dt^2}$ \textit{with respect to} $\{y^I\}$.

Accordingly, one may state:

\begin{definition}
    \textbf{PIN. v.3}:  \textit{Relative to} an inertial reference frame (as defined above in Def. \eqref{INRF}), bodies maintain their state of inertial motion (rest or uniform motion) or accelerate according to Newton's second law. \label{PIN3}
\end{definition}

However, this reformulation still depends on the very concept it aims to define, namely inertia, thereby reintroducing the original circularity. A reevaluation of how we define both inertial motion and inertial reference frames is therefore necessary.

\begin{comment}
To conclude, I stress that the reliance on force-based definitions of inertia introduces some additional complications:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[(i)] \textit{If} gravity is understood as a force --- and it \textit{is} understood as a force in those definitions of inertial frames typically adopted in standard versions of Newtonian gravity--- a truly force-free system is physically unattainable due to the omnipresence of gravitational interactions, which have infinite range and cannot be nullified except at an idealised infinity. However, as \cite{Hilbert_1984} aptly noted, “the infinite is nowhere to be found in reality, no matter what experiences, observations, and knowledge are appealed to.”
Note also that this force-based definition contradicts the more fundamental nature of gravity as we know it today, which, as per GR, is \textit{not} a force but a manifestation of spacetime curvature. Consequently, this notions of inertia would become untenable within GR (I will address the definition of inertia in the relativistic context in \S\ref{PROPOSAL}). Certainly, an object can have an equal and opposite reaction to that of gravity. However, I believe that saying that no forces act on a body (as the inertial motion requires) is different from saying that forces act on a body whose total resultant cancels out.

    \item[(ii)] On the other hand, \textit{if} gravity were not understood as a force, the concept of inertia as a force-free state fails to account for scenarios where objects, while commonly understood as stationary in an \textbf{INRF} (e.g., resting on a table), \textit{are} subject to forces (e.g., normal forces countering gravity). Such objects, are commonly understood as inertial objects: a pen rests on a table if no forces act on it. However, though seemingly at rest, these objects \textit{do} experience forces counteracting gravity.

\end{enumerate}

\end{comment}


\subsection{Principle or Law?}\label{lawvsprin}

We should ask: is inertia an empirical law that describes observed regularities, or a principle taken as a starting point to account for those regularities?\footnote{In the first case scenario, another topic of debate is whether the \textit{Law of inertia} is a separate law or a corollary to the Newton's Second Law. In essence, the answers depends on the formulation of the Newtonian theory.
In a four‐dimensional (covariant) formulation, once inertial frames are properly defined as adapted to the assumed flat affine structure, the first law follows from the second law since it merely encodes that force-free particles follow geodesics (i.e. “straight lines” in space–time).
On the other hand, in the standard three-dimensional formulation, \lq{}\lq{}the first law asserts that there exist certain frames with respect to which the second law is supposed to hold. The second law thus does not even make sense without the first law to define those frames\rq{}\rq{} \cite[p.7]{JacobsForthcoming-JACHNT-2}. See the discussion below for details.
}

Until now, I referred to the \lq{}principle of inertia\rq{} and not the \lq{}law of inertia\rq{}.
Let me therefore clarify the two terms by introducing a\textit{ law-like terminology} and a \textit{principle-like terminology}.


\paragraph{The Law of Inertia: The Law-like Terminology.}
In Newtonian mechanics the “law of inertia” is traditionally expressed by Newton’s first law – a statement that free (force‑free) bodies continue in uniform straight‑line motion (cf, Def. \eqref{PIN1}) – and is regarded as an \textit{empirical law} on a par with Newton's other laws.
In more detail, in the law‑like terminology, Newton’s first law is an empirical statement: free bodies move uniformly in straight lines.

The common ground for the law‑like terminology is the standard \textit{three-dimensional framework}, where inertial frames are defined as those relative to which the simplest form of Newton’s laws holds; that is, they are frames where free particles appear to move uniformly $(v=const)$ and forced particles follow $F=ma$ (see \citealp{Weatherall2020-WEATDO-7} for a critique on the use of simplicity as a criterion).
The three-dimensional framework is in line with what \cite{JacobsForthcoming-JACHNT-2} call the \textit{law-based approach} on inertia where the first law is used to \textit{define} inertial frames (presupposing they exists).\footnote{This need to presuppose the existence of what one defines not only is methodologically problematic, but also undermines the definition of inertia as an empirical law in the same way as Newton's other laws. See \citealp[p.8]{bergmann1976introduction}. To be clear, this was not a problem for Newton himself, since he defined its first law with respect to Absolute space. Thus, according to Newton its first law was an empirical statement about the motion of bodies and not a definition of inertial frames, nor a statement for their existence.}
However, as already stressed above, a law‑based account is problematic because it tends to be circular: one identifies inertial frames by the very behaviour (inertial motion) that the law is meant to explain. Furthermore, arbitrary coordinate transformations can always “hide” or “recover” the formal simplicity of the laws, making the definition too liberal.

In response,\textit{ the four-dimensional (covariant) framework} can be adopted, which is more in line with what \cite{JacobsForthcoming-JACHNT-2} call the \textit{structure‑based approach}. Here, inertial frames are those adapted to the underlying flat affine structure of spacetime (\textit{Galilean Spacetime} in the case of Newtonian physics \citealp{Earman1992-jn}), thus grounding the Law of Inertia in the geometry of the world rather than in particle dynamics. In this setting, the law of inertia does note \textit{define} the inertial frames, which are instead defined independently in terms of the presupposed flat-affine structure.\footnote{Rigorously, an affine structure is provided by an affine connection (not necessarily the \textit{unique Levi-Civita connection} of a metric). This connection tells you how to parallel-transport vectors and defines the geodesics of the manifold.
In certain coordinates, called affine or Riemann-normal coordinate, we can define a \textit{flat} affine spacetime that has \textit{all} Levi-Civita coefficients equal to zero.
Importantly, if it is possible to find a coordinate system where all Levi-Civita coefficients vanish everywhere, then the Riemann curvature tensor must be identically zero, and this fact remains true in all coordinate systems. However, in a different coordinate system, some Levi-Civita coefficients may become non-zero, even though the affine structure remains flat. Thus, in Newtonian spacetime this structure allows for the existence of \textit{global} inertial frames related by Galilean transformations (rotations, spatial translations, time translations, and boosts). For completeness, let me stress that another important geometric structure is the \textit{conformal structure}, which in GR is governed by the Weyl tensor. The conformal structure in Newtonian spacetime is less straightforward than in relativistic settings due to its degenerate metrics. While the Riemann tensor in Newtonian gravity shares algebraic symmetries with its relativistic counterpart, the Weyl tensor has no a direct analogue, as Newtonian curvature lacks vacuum/conformal degrees of freedom.
This reflects the fundamental distinction between Newtonian gravity (elliptic, non-radiative) and GR (hyperbolic, radiative). Advanced extensions (e.g., \textit{teleparallel Newton-Cartan} \citep{Schwartz2023}) explore alternative formulations, but the core absence of a Weyl-like structure persists.
However, for lovers of twists and turns, in Newtonian gravity it is possible to define the \lq{}\textit{electric tidal tensor}’ $E_{ij}$, as the trace-free Hessian of the gravitational potential $\Phi$: $$E_{ij}=\cfrac{\partial^2\Phi}{\partial x^i \partial x^j}-\cfrac{1}{3}\delta_{ij}\nabla^2\Phi.$$
This object serves as the Newtonian analogue of the electric part of the relativistic Weyl tensor since it describes gravity when $\nabla^2\Phi=0$ (i.e. in vacuum). In GR the Weyl tensor $C_{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}$ splits into \textit{electric} $(E_{\mu\nu})$ and \textit{magnetic} $(B_{\mu\nu})$ parts. The electric Weyl tensor $E_{\mu\nu}$ governs tidal forces, analogous to $E_{ij}$ in Newtonian gravity.  Mathematically, $E_{ij}$ matches the spatial components of $E_{\mu\nu}$ in a slow-motion, weak-field regime. 
However, Newtonian gravity lacks a counterpart to the magnetic Weyl tensor, as there are no propagating degrees of freedom (e.g., gravitational waves) or frame-dragging effects. Furthermore, $E_{ij}$ is fully determined by the instantaneous mass distribution (via $\Phi$), unlike in GR, where the Weyl tensor evolves dynamically even in vacuum. In this sense, Newtonian theory lacks a \textit{full} Weyl structure.} 
In particular—as emphasised in \cite{Earman1973}'s treatment—the law of inertia can be recast geometrically: free particles follow \textit{geodesics} of the fixed \textit{flat}-affine structure of Newtonian space‑time.

\textit{Nota Bene}: the structure-based approach still accords with what I call the law-like terminology for inertia, which aims to define a \textit{law} of inertia. Do not confuse \cite{JacobsForthcoming-JACHNT-2}'s law-based approach with my law-like terminology.




\paragraph{The Principle of Inertia: the principle-like terminology.}

In\cite{JacobsForthcoming-JACHNT-2} one finds a \textit{symmetry-based} definition of \textbf{INRFs}, which suggests interpreting inertia as a principle rather than a law.
In contrast to law-based or structure-based definitions, here an \textbf{INRF} is defined as a frame that \lq{}\lq{}\textit{meshes with the dynamical symmetries of Newton theory} (ivi, p.2)\rq{}\rq{}.
Thus, in line with \cite{GomesSymmetries},  Jacobs supports the view that dynamics and reference frames are \lq{}a package deal\rq{}: the dynamical content of a theory is provided together with a class of allowed reference frames that are consistent with the dynamical symmetries and, for \cite{Earman1992-jn}'s \textbf{SP} principles, with the spacetime symmetries that leave the spatiotemporal background unchanged.

In detail, following the frequent misuse of the terms reference frame and coordinate system as interchangeable, Jacobs defines:\footnote{In literature, the most comprehensive analysis on this issue of the distinction between the concept of \lq{}coordinate\rq{} and \lq{}reference frame\rq{} can be found in \cite{Bamonti2023}. In this work I follow the more cautious position of \textit{not} using the terms interchangeably, as Jacobs did.}
\begin{quote}
    \textbf{Inertial coordinate system (symmetry-based)}: a coordinate system that is adapted to a symmetry-invariant metric, \textit{and in which force-free bodies move with constant (coordinate) velocity.} [my italics] (ivi, p. 22).
\end{quote}
To be clear: whereas the law-based and structure-based approaches support what I called a \emph{law-like} terminology for inertia, the symmetry-based framework instead lends itself to a \emph{principle-like} reading, rooted in the symmetry principles articulated by Earman.

Several remarks are in order.

First, as already noted above and well understood since at least \cite{einstein2015relativity}, the term \emph{force} is itself defined in terms of deviation from inertial motion. It cannot, therefore, appear in a non-circular definition of inertia: to do so would be to assume the very notion one is attempting to define.

Second, I contend that Jacobs’ characterisation is, at best, a necessary condition for inertiality, but not a sufficient one. A reference frame that is \emph{dynamically uncoupled} from the system it describes may still satisfy the above definition, but it fails to “mesh” with the full spatiotemporal structure—both dynamical and geometric—of the theory. Jacobs’ proposal more accurately characterises the notion of \emph{dynamical coupling} rather than that of inertia proper.\footnote{See \cite{BamontiGomes2024,BamontiGomes2024PARTII} for a complete overview of what dynamically (un)coupled means. Briefly, in such frameworks—where both the reference frame and the target system are modelled as fields—a frame is dynamically uncoupled if one can act with a dynamical symmetry on either component independently, while preserving solutionhood.}


Despite these criticisms, Jacobs’ account proves useful for the purposes of the present discussion. Building upon his formulation, I argue that reinterpreting inertia not merely as a “law” but rather as a “principle” becomes viable precisely through the adoption of a symmetry-based perspective, grounded in Earman’s \textbf{SP} \textit{principles}.
When one refers to the “principle of inertia,” the emphasis shifts from empirical generalisation (as in law-based or structure-based formulations) to a more profound conceptual foundation. In this view, the principle is not merely descriptive—it does not simply report that force-free bodies move in straight lines—but rather prescriptive: it postulates a fundamental constraint on \emph{both} the structure of space (or spacetime) and the dynamics of physical theories. It thereby assumes the role of a constitutive principle, shaping what counts as “natural motion”. (I shall argue in \S\ref{deviation} that even this interpretation is ultimately problematic.)

Understood as such, the principle of inertia becomes a foundational theoretical postulate—an organising idea that informs the formulation of physical theories. This perspective is particularly salient in the transition from Newtonian mechanics to GR, where Newton’s first law is subsumed by the \emph{geodesic principle}, 





\section{The Einstein's Law of Inertia} \label{geodesic}

Let me now turn the discussion to the relativistic case of GR. This turn does not mean abandoning the classical case, in fact the results I will obtain in GR regarding the concept of inertia will be more general and naturally extendable to the classical Newtonian case under appropriate limits.

In GR, one naturally adopts a \emph{structural} view: gravity is not a force but rather encoded in the curvature of spacetime.  Consequently, departures from inertial motion cannot be ascribed to gravitational forces, unlike in Newtonian formulations (whether in three‑ or four‑dimensional formulation).
For completeness, I point out that this geometrical understanding is not exclusive to GR, but already appears in non-relativistic settings like Newton–Cartan theory (NCT), where one “geometrises” the gravitational interaction (see \citealp[\S3]{Earman1973}, \citealp{Trautman1965-TRALOG,Trautman1967}, or \citealp[\S4]{James_Read2023-mk} for a general but concise review of NCT).


I propose the following revised version of Definition \eqref{INERTIA1}:

\begin{definition}
    \textbf{Inertial Motion. v.2}: A body undergoes inertial motion if and only if it moves along a geodesic of the \textit{unique} Levi–Civita connection associated with the spacetime metric, \emph{i.e.} it is in\textit{ free fall} in a gravitational field. \label{INERTIA2}
\end{definition}

Freely falling bodies follow geodesics, which are the deemed as the \lq{}natural trajectories\rq{} in curved spacetime. These geodesics define inertial motion.
Crucially, this holds \textit{locally, along the geodesic} (more on that in \S\ref{EQUIVALENCE}).
Put in practical and easily visualisable terms, motion is inertial for the falling object not for those who ‘watch it fall’ from a reference frame that itself does not fall (i.e. are not \textit{comoving}).

Equivalently, inertial motion can be associated with a \textit{locally flat} Levi-Civita connection, that is:  its coefficients $\Gamma^a_{bc}$ are \textit{locally nullifiable}.\footnote{To be rigorous, it is an abuse of notation to write the components of the Levi-Civita connection in abstract index notation, since $\Gamma^a_{bc}$ is only made explicit once a coordinate basis has been chosen. Under a change of basis (or coordinate transformation) the connection coefficients do not transform as tensors; they acquire an additional inhomogeneous term, which only cancels for affine transformations. It is then said that (the coefficients of) affine connections are \textit{pseudo-tensors}, i.e. objects that are tensorial only for affine transformations. One should distinguish these coefficients from the abstract connection operator $\nabla$.}

Definition \eqref{INERTIA2} vindicates the fact that an object at rest on Earth's surface is \textit{not} in inertial motion, even if it would commonly be understood as inertial according to Definition \eqref{INERTIA1}: a pen at rest on a table continue to remain at rest, if no forces act on it.
However, this objects \textit{does} experience a normal force counteracting gravity.
Indeed, an accelerometer at rest on a table records a non‑zero acceleration precisely because the table prevents the object from following its geodesic.\footnote{By this I do not mean that I have revealed a fact that has never been recognised in the physics of gravity. The attentive GR reader is well aware that \citealp[p.67]{Wald93} already stressed exactly this point.}
By contrast, a dropped object—deprived of any non‑gravitational influence—follows a geodesic and is truly inertial.

Einstein himself anticipated this view when he described “the law of motion of General Relativity” as follows:
\begin{quote}
[\dots] a gravitating particle moves in a geodesic line. This constitutes a hypothetical translation of Galileo’s law of inertia to the case of the existence of ‘genuine’ gravitational fields \cite[p.113]{Einstein1922}.
\end{quote}
Notably, Einstein did not restrict attention to infinitesimal test particles but envisaged arbitrarily massive bodies—a point to which we shall return in \S\ref{TESTPARTICLE}.\footnote{See also \cite{Einstein1916,Einstein1922}.  A remarkable example of how this notion of inertial motion was also applied to extended bodies and not just point-like ones is the description of Mercury's perihelion motion as worked out by Einstein.} 
Standard expositions of GR likewise adopt the \emph{geodesic principle}—that freely falling test bodies satisfy the geodesic equation—as the relativistic analogue of inertial motion \citep{misner2017gravitation,Wald1984}.\footnote{Geodesic motion as inertial motion is also valid in NCT. Here, we can define a curved affine connection $\nabla^{NCT}$ compatible with spatial and temporal metrics such that the trajectories of gravitationally interacting particles are geodesics of $\nabla_{NCT}$: $\nabla^{NCT}_{u^a}u^a=0$. However, recall that inertial motion is \textit{only} that with zero Levi-Civita coefficients. Therefore, by geometrising gravity one recovers the possibility of \textit{local inertial frames}: i.e. reference frames where the Levi-Civita coefficients cancel \textit{at a point} and the motion is \textit{locally} inertial, exactly like in GR (see \cite{Weatherall2011} on the status of the geodesic principle in NCT).\label{NCT}}



Significantly, Definition \eqref{INERTIA2}, excludes the case of curvilinear, but not curved, spacetime.
It is essential to distinguish \emph{curved} from merely \emph{curvilinear} coordinate effects.
Inertial motion is well‑defined only when the Riemann tensor $R^{a}{}_{bcd}\neq0$, signalling genuine spacetime curvature.
On the contrary, in the case I define as curvilinear, the (coefficients of the) Levi-Civita connections are non-null but the Riemann is null.\footnote{In the remainder of the paper, for the sake of simplicity, I will sometimes not distinguish between the Levi-Civita connection $\nabla$ and its coefficients $\Gamma^a_{bc}$ and will say that \lq{}the Levi-Civita connection is nullifiable locally\rq{}. The reader knows that we can't make $\nabla=0$ --- the connection remains a nontrivial \textit{operator}. What we nullify is its representation  $\Gamma$ at a point by choosing the right coordinate basis.} The presence of \textit{curved} Levi-Civita connections (that is: its coefficients are non-null) and a null Riemann identifies the presence of \textit{non-inertial, fictitious} forces. An object in a field of non-inertial forces (i.e. non-gravitational in the classical sense) is not in inertial motion.
Still it remains true that these forces, which are coded in the Levi-Civita connections, are removable pointwise, so that the inertial motion is restored.


One might object that, in the absence of gravity ($R^{a}{}_{bcd}=0$), the definition of inertial motion as gravitational motion collapses. 
%This also applies to the case of curvilinear space mentioned above, where I have just said that it is possible to recover a notion of local inertial motion.
The best way to answer this critique was already given by \cite{einstein2015relativity}'s famous quote:\lq{}If we imagine the gravitational field to be removed there does remain absolute \textit{nothing}, and also no topological space.'\rq{}.
Thus,  there is no “gravity‑free spacetime” to which one could apply a separate notion of inertia and the question proves to be a physical non-sense.

%Below, in \S\eqref{EQUIVALENCE} I will also suggest that inertial, geodesic (that is, gravitational) motion is \textit{different} from non-inertial motion. Their often being understood as equivalent forms the basis of the equivalence principle, which, however, is subject to numerous misunderstandings.


In light of Definition \eqref{INERTIA2}, the revised Principle of Inertia \eqref{PIN1} becomes:

\begin{definition}
    \textbf{PIN. v.4}: A body maintains inertial motion precisely when its only interaction is gravitational, so that its worldline is a geodesic. \label{PIN4}
\end{definition}

Although this definition eliminates the circularity inherent in the precedent versions, it merely restates the nature of geodesic motion as the motion under exclusive gravitational influence.
So, if not circular, Definition \ref{PIN4} seems \textit{trivial} as it would read as: \lq{}\lq{}\textit{Bodies maintain inertial motion if they they move along geodesics, which define inertial motion}\rq{}\rq{}.

As done for Definition \eqref{PIN3}, to regain substance, one introduces the concept of a \emph{local inertial reference frame} in the relativistic setting.
In particular, along the lines of Definition \ref{INRF} we can define:\footnote{A different, \textit{non-operational in spirit}, definition of a \textit{global} inertial reference frame in GR is given in \cite{Earman1973}:
\begin{quote}
A generalized inertial frame in a relativistic space-time is determined
by a timelike vector field $X$ such that (i) $X$ is a Killing field (ii) the
trajectories of $X$ are hypersurface orthogonal and (iii) each pair of
hypersurfaces is everywhere a constant space-time distance apart as
measured along the trajectories of $X$.
\end{quote}
In particular, condition (iii) means that the proper time between any two 3-hypersurfaces, measured along $X$, is uniform everywhere. This eliminates the possibility of gravitational time dilation. As a consequence, the metric can be written as $ds^2=-dt^2+g_{ij}dx^idx^j$ which corresponds to the case in which the \textit{lapse function} (relating coordinate time to proper time) is constant and equal to 1 \citep{Wald1984}.
So, this definition restricts the possibility of global inertial frames to \textit{ultrastatic spacetimes}, which are highly symmetric. In such spacetimes observers following $X$ are geodesic observers and gravitational effects like time dilation and frame-dragging are absent, allowing global synchronisation and disallowing non-rotating observers \citep{Landau1987-fh}.
The geodesic equations are compatible with the isometries generated by $X$. For example, in Minkowski spacetime (which is flat and ultrastatic), geodesics are straight lines in the inertial frame, matching the trajectories of $X$-observers. In curved ultrastatic spacetimes (e.g. $\Sigma^3 \times \mathbb{R}$, where $\Sigma^3$ is a curved spatial manifold), geodesics follow the spatial curvature of $\Sigma^3$.

For an analysis of this way of defining a reference frame, not instantiated by physical objects but by the spacetime structure, see \cite{Bamonti2023}.}
\begin{definition}
    \textbf{(Local) INRF. v.2}: A \textit{local} inertial reference frame provides a \textit{local} standard of measurement for space and time $\{x^{I}\}$ in which the geodesic equation \begin{equation}
\frac{D^2 x^a}{d\tau^2} = \frac{d^2 x^a}{d\tau^2} + \Gamma^a_{bc} \frac{dx^b}{d\tau} \frac{dx^c}{d\tau} = 0
\end{equation} reduces to \begin{equation}
\frac{D^2 x^I}{d\tau^2} = \frac{d^2 x^I}{d\tau^2}= 0 \quad (\text{with }\Gamma^I_{JK}=0\text{ locally}).
\end{equation} In such a frame, any deviation from this equation arises exclusively from non-gravitational causes. \label{INRF2}
\end{definition}

This formulation underscores that inertiality in GR is a \textit{local} concept, determined by whether free particles follow geodesic trajectories of a \textit{locally} null Levi-Civita connection.
Note also that the \textit{local} \textbf{INRF} is a \textit{comoving frame}, it does not need to be a \textit{synchronous frame} \citep{Landau1987-fh, Bamonti2024}.
That is, the proper time $\tau$ measured by an observer in free fall does not have to coincide with the clock parameter $x^4$ of the local \textbf{INRF} adapted to the observer in free fall.
To understand why, just remember that it is possible to parameterise a geodesic with a generic affine parameter $\lambda = \alpha\tau + \beta,$ $(\alpha \neq 0,\, \beta \in \mathbb{R})$, which has no direct interpretation as time measured by some clock (see the \textit{clock hypothesis} in \citealp{malament2012topics}).

%A concrete example of a \textit{local inertial reference frame} in GR could be instantiated by a set of four non-backreacting GPS satellites \citep{RovelliGPS}, broadcasting their proper time measurements to $p\in U$, and which follow the geodesics of a flat-affine connection compatible with the local Minkowskian metric. Agreed: this means imposing \textit{in advance} that the affine structure is flat. A purely operational construction of an inertial system, i.e. a system for which the Christoffels are zero (even if only locally or along a geodesic), is an interesting theoretical challenge that requires separate work.%\footnote{Note that \cite{Bamonti2023} deals with material reference frames. Whether an inertial reference frame is instantiated by some material system or not does not matter for the topic of the paper. In any case, Bamonti is aware of the possibility of gravitational reference frames, such as Komar-Kretschmann scalars \citep{Komar, GomesRepresentationalscheme}. I will show later in \S\ref{EQUIVALENCE} and \S\ref{TESTPARTICLE} that in the real world there are no concrete inertial frames over finite regions of spacetime. This suggests that it is not possible to construct a notion of an inertial reference frame operationally, either in the \lq{}\textit{weak sense}\rq{} of operationalism, where definitions are simply required to have an empirical basis, i.e. to be ultimately related to the world of measurement, without the need to describe in detail what the specific operations are, or in the \lq{}\textit{stronger sense}\rq{} \textit{à la} \cite{bridgman1936nature}, where the experimental measurement procedures must be made explicit.}

 I will show later in \S\ref{EQUIVALENCE} and \S\ref{TESTPARTICLE} that in nature there are no concrete inertial frames over finite regions of spacetime. This suggests that it is not possible to construct a notion of an inertial reference frame operationally, either in the \lq{}\textit{weak sense}\rq{} of operationalism, where definitions are simply required to have an empirical basis, i.e. to be ultimately related to the world of measurement, without the need to describe in detail what the specific operations are, or in the \lq{}\textit{stronger sense}\rq{} \textit{à la} \cite{bridgman1936nature}, where the experimental measurement procedures must be made explicit.


Crucially, Definition \eqref{INRF2} does not imply that \textit{any} reference frame is \textit{always} inertial simply because gravity is the only interaction present.
Even in purely gravitational settings, non‑inertial frames arise.  Consider three observers in the exterior of a Schwarzschild black hole of mass $M$:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Alice}: freely falling radially, so that she satisfies: $\frac{d^2 x^I}{d\tau^2} = 0$, with Levi-Civita coefficients  \( \Gamma^I_{JK} = 0 \) along her geodesics. This defines Alice's \textit{local inertial frame} $\{x^I\}$.\footnote{\lq{}\lq{} [\dots] the idea of inertial reference frames [\dots] are examples of [\cite{Bamonti2023}'s] \textit{dynamical reference frames}.\rq{}\rq{} \cite[fn.13]{bamontithebault}. Dynamical reference frames are reference frames in which the set of equations that determine the dynamics of the matter field is included but the the stress-energy tensor of the matter field used as reference frame is neglected.}
    \item \textbf{Bob}: held stationary at Schwarzschild radius $r_0$ by a rocket, experiences proper acceleration measured by an onboard accelerometer to counteract the gravitational pull of the black hole ($r_0$ is the distance from the singularity as defined by an observer at infinity, named Carl). In a sense, Bob is akin to the above-mentioned pen held stationary on Earth. Bob's non-inertiality manifests in the geodesic equation he assigns \textit{to Alice}:
\begin{equation}
    \frac{d^2 y^I}{d\tau^2} + \Gamma^I_{JK} \frac{dx^J}{d\tau} \frac{dx^K}{d\tau} = 0.
\end{equation}
That is, Bob's non-inertial frame $\{y^I\}$ introduce fictitious forces $\Gamma^{I}{}_{JK}\neq 0$ \textit{to Alice's motion}, mirroring Bob's proper acceleration.
    \item \textbf{Carl}: situated at spatial asymptotic infinity (which is Minkowskian), defining a  a \textit{global inertial frame} $\{z^I\}:=\{t_C,r_C,\theta_C,\phi_C\}$, which is the well-known Schwarzschild frame.\footnote{In standard textbooks, Schwarzschild's are coordinates, not reference frames. In this case, since Carl is an asymptotic observer, his physical role is idealised. So, to be precise, $\{z^I\}$ is a (global) \textit{idealised reference frame} in \cite{Bamonti2023}'s terms. Again from Bamonti (\S4) we learn that, in practice, idealised reference frames and coordinates play the same role: this is why they are often conflated.} He describes Alice's radial \textit{free-fall} motion by:
\begin{equation}
    \frac{dr_C}{dt_C} = -\left(1 - \frac{2M}{r_C}\right)\sqrt{\frac{2M}{r_C}},
\end{equation}
while Bob's trajectory is described by \( dr_C/dt_C = 0 \) (since Bob’s position is fixed at $r_C=r_0$).
\end{itemize}



This picture I provided highlights that geodesic equation encodes \textit{both} physical motion and observer-dependent effects. Thus, while gravity manifests as geodesic motion in curved spacetime, non-inertial frames persist due to physical acceleration or non-locally adapted frames.  

%For instance, Bob, a stationary observer resisting gravity, measures non-zero proper acceleration and observes geodesic motion (e.g., Alice’s free-fall) as accelerated trajectories with non-zero Christoffel symbols. Conversely, Carl, an infinitely distant observer in asymptotically flat spacetime, operates in a global inertial frame where Christoffel symbols vanish. This distinction underscores that spacetime curvature alone does not guarantee inertiality; proper acceleration (Bob) or coordinate choice (Schwarzschild vs. Fermi-normal) determines whether a frame is inertial. Thus, while gravity manifests as geodesic motion in curved spacetime, non-inertial frames persist due to physical acceleration or non-adapted coordinates.


%In fact, even if gravity is the only (relevant) interaction to consider, apparent accelerations (or fictitious forces) may occur, that make the geodesic equation of a body manifest in its standard form with the affine connections $\Gamma^a_{bc}\neq 0$, thus defining a non-inertial frame. Such non-inertial frame is accelerating relative to the free-falling (inertial) \textit{local} frame $\{x^I\}$, so that bodies no longer appear to follow the geodesic equation $\frac{D^2x^I}{d\tau^2}=0$ with respect to that inertial frame. An example is a stationary observer in Schwarzschild Spacetime. In the observer's frame (call them Bob), a body (Alice) falling toward the black hole does follow a geodesic path with a generic Christoffel. In short, while Alice's trajectory is a geodesic in spacetime (when described in a frame appropriate for free-falling observers or using Schwarzschild time, which corresponds to the proper time of an observer at infinity), her path \textit{relative to Bob's frame} does not appear geodesic. 



Analogously to Definition \eqref{PIN3}, Definition \ref{PIN4} may be restated:
\begin{definition}
    \textbf{PIN. v.5}: \textit{Relative to} a \textit{local} inertial reference frame (as defined in \ref{INRF2}), a body maintains its geodesic motion $d^2x^I/d\tau^2=0$.\label{PIN5}
\end{definition}

As it is easy to check, the triviality also dooms Definition \ref{PIN5}, if it is the case that the motion due to gravitational interaction alone is geodesic motion.
This invites further refinement.
The path taken, which is based on the geodesic principle, does not seem to be the right one for a good definition of inertia.


\noindent We may still have an ace up our sleeve.

In certain spacetimes—specifically, those with torsion—the notion of geodesics (often called \textit{self-parallel curves}) does not necessarily coincide with the notion of curves that extremise the spatiotemporal distance (often called \textit{extremal curves}).
This discrepancy arises because, unlike in \lq{}standard GR\rq{}, the Levi-Civita connections and the more general affine connections are distinct in these settings.

\textit{Levi-Civita connections} are defined in terms of the gravitational field and are used to express the geodesic equation in spacetimes without torsion; they are symmetric in their lower indices.

In contrast, \textit{(general) affine connections} are defined without reference to a gravitational field and need not be symmetric. The antisymmetric part of an affine connection defines the \textit{torsion field}.
In spacetimes with torsion, the affine connection is used to define extremal curves, which may differ from the self-parallel (geodesic) curves determined by the Levi-Civita connections in the torsion-free case.\footnote{Levi-Civita connections, respecting the compatibility condition $\nabla_cg_{ab}=0$ (also known as \textit{Ricci Theorem} in tensor analysis), are related to the derivatives of the metric. Their coefficients are also sometimes referred to as \textit{Christoffell's symbols} $\Gamma^a_{bc} = \frac{1}{2} \, g^{ad} \left( \partial_b g_{dc} + \partial_c g_{bd} - \partial_d g_{bc} \right)$. Affine connections and Christoﬀell symbols (i.e. Levi-Civita coefficients) in principle represent different objects. Affine connections are defined as the components of the derivative of the basis vectors along the tangent directions of the manifold itself (the component normal to the manifold is called the \textit{extrinsic curvature}). Thus, to define a generic affine connection, we do not need a metric structure on the manifold.
Christoﬀell symbols, on the other hand, are defined from metric properties of the manifold by introducing the metric tensor.}


In the case of a free-falling \textit{spinning} test particle in a gravitational field with torsion, the question arises: does the free-falling particle follow the trajectory of an an extremal curve or a geodesic curve?
Does it extremise the distance between two points or does it keep the covariant derivative of the tangent vector equal to zero?
This question could lead us to understand inertial motion as motion along extremal curves rather than along geodesics, which would also remove triviality from the Definition \eqref{PIN5}, since inertial motion would not coincide with geodesic motion.

Unfortunately, this is not how things work \citep[p.34]{Weatherall2016-WEAIME}.
The equations of motion of spinning particles obey \textit{Mathisson–Papapetrou–Dixon equations} which include spin–torsion interactions (\citealp{Papa1951,TRAUTMAN1973,Trautman:2006fp, Blagojevi2011}).\footnote{Other relativistic theories involving torsion are Teleparallel Gravity (TEGR) \citep{Aldrovandi2013}, $f(T)$ gravity \citep{Cai2016}, Poincaré Gauge Theory (PGT) \citep{Blagojevi2003ThreeLO}, Metric-Affine gravity \citep{Hehl1995}.
The generalised Mathisson--Papapetrou--Dixon (MPD) equations in Einstein--Cartan theory are given by (see \citealp{Pei1990}):
\textbf{Force Equation:}
$$
\frac{D p^a}{D\tau} = -\frac{1}{2}\,\tilde{R}^a{}_{bcd}\,u^b\,S^{cd} - K^a{}_{cd}\,\frac{D S^{cd}}{D\tau},
$$
\textbf{Spin Equation:}
$$
\frac{D S^{ab}}{D\tau} = 2\,p^{[a}\,u^{b]} + 2\,K^{[a}{}_{cd}\,S^{b]d}\,u^c.
$$
Here, we denote: $\frac{D}{D\tau}$ as the covariant derivative along the test particle's worldline parametrised by proper time $\tau$, $p^a$ as the particle's 4-momentum,
$u^a$ as the particle's four-velocity,
$S^{ab}$ as the antisymmetric spin tensor,
$\tilde{R}^a{}_{bcd}$ as the curvature tensor computed from the full affine connection (including torsion),
$K^a{}_{cd}$ as the \textit{contortion tensor}, which is related to the torsion $\Gamma^a{}_{[cd]}=\frac{1}{2}\bigl(\Gamma^a{}_{cd} - \Gamma^a{}_{dc}\bigr)$ (with $\Gamma^a_{cd}$ the usual symmetric Levi-Civita connection) by
$K^a{}_{cd} = \frac{1}{2} \left( \Gamma^a{}_{[cd]} - \Gamma^b{}_{[ca]} g_{d b} + \Gamma^b{}_{[d a]} g_{cb} \right)$.
The notation $[cd]$ denotes antisymmetrisation in the indices.\label{fnMDP}}
In such theories—\emph{e.g.} Einstein–Cartan theory \citep{Cartan1922,Hehl1995,Penrose1983}—neither autoparallels nor extremal curves alone capture the true inertial trajectories, further challenging any simple geodesic-based definition of inertia.

\begin{comment}
This allows us to define inertial motion by avoiding explicit reference to geodesic --- that is gravitational --- motion.
We can redefine inertial motion as follows:

\begin{definition}
    \textbf{Inertial Motion v.3}: It is the motion that minimises the travelled spatiotemporal distance.\label{INERTIA3}
\end{definition}

Accordingly, the principle of inertia takes the following form:

\begin{definition}
    \textbf{PIN v.6}: Bodies maintain inertial motion by following trajectories of minimal spatiotemporal distance. \label{PIN5} 
\end{definition}


Analogously as done above, we can also reframe Definition \eqref{PIN5} by adopting an appropriately defined notion of an inertial reference frame.

We introduce:

