\paragraph{An Aside on Definition 6: Revisiting Classical Perspectives.}\label{NEWTON}


So far we have said that an object in free fall is, locally, in inertial motion, pointing out some critical aspects of defining inertial motion via geodesic motion.

This description of inertia does not make use of the concept of gravity as a force since in relativistic terms, the concept of gravitational force is problematic as it assumes action at a distance.


At this point, an intriguing question arises: if gravity is \textit{not} a force, why can we accurately predict the motion of falling bodies using Newton’s second law, which treats gravity as a force (both in its 3D and 4D formulation
Recall that in the standard Newtonian viewpoint this gravitational force on an object placed on the Earth's surface is balanced by the force the surface exerts, leaving the body in equilibrium, i.e., "at rest." 
This classical description, while conceptually outdated within the framework of GR, remains \textit{operationally effective}. 
Bridges, roads, highways and even rockets are built with Newtonian physics.

I believe the answer lies in a \lq{}shift of classical perspective\rq{}.
Within the standard Newtonian framework we assign an acceleration to the body in free fall, interpreting gravity as the force acting on the body and causing its acceleration.
In contrast, inspired by GR, we could treat the body in free fall as moving inertially without \textit{any} force acting on it, while the observer on the surface of the Earth experiences an acceleration due to the non-inertial nature of their reference frame.

Despite differing interpretations, the predictions are exactly the same.
It is the understanding of the classical gravitational phenomenon that fundamentally changes.

This change of perspective reconciles classical and relativistic gravity, understanding gravity not as a force and the gravitational motion as the \textit{inertial motion}.

In other words, the gravitational force is introduced only because we choose to regard a state or sequence of states as 'unnatural', and the force is postulated to explain the unnaturalness.
Thus, in Newtonian gravitation, we usually see the falling of bodies as an accelerated (unnatural) motion, a consequence of the action of a force --- gravity --- disrupting their natural inertial motion.
However, as I have shown, it is possible to change the conventional perspective and interpret the falling of bodies in a different way.
Conventionality must be understood in the same sense as in Reichenbach's work: if two theories give the same observational predictions, then the choice between them is a matter of convention, based on descriptive simplicity, elegance, and so on.  The choice between them does not affect the content of our knowledge of the world.
%\footnote{It is similar to Ellis's position that what counts as natural motion is a matter of convention. However, it does not quite coincide, since Ellis argues that the two perspectives support '\textit{different theoretical commitments}' (ivi, p. 51).}
\end{comment}


\subsection{The Link With the Equivalence Principle: What is Local?}\label{EQUIVALENCE}
According to Definition \eqref{INERTIA2}, inertial motion is identified with the geodesic motion of a curved Levi-Civita connection $\nabla_g$ which is \textit{locally flat}, namely its components $\Gamma^a_{bc}$ can be made to vanish at a point.
This accords with the standard claim that "spacetime is locally flat" and that "special relativity holds locally".\footnote{See \citealp{Brown2016,Read2018,GomesSR,Fletcher2023-FLETLV,Teh2024} for discussions on the validity of this claim. \label{fnlocalsr}}
In practice, one often states that local inertial motion follows from
\begin{equation}
\frac{d^2x^a}{d\tau^2}=0
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\Gamma^{a}{}_{bc}=0,
\end{equation}
and indeed the geodesic equation is frequently derived from the Equivalence Principle (EP) \citep[§1.4]{ferrari2020general}.\footnote{In this work I will always refer to what in philosophical literature is called the \textit{Strong Equivalent Principle}. There are three equivalence principles: the Weak Equivalence Principle (WEP) concerning the equivalence between inertial mass and gravitational mass, the Einstein Equivalence Principle (EEP) which addresses the \textit{identity of gravity and inertia} (ontological equivalence) and the Strong Equivalence Principle (SEP) which addresses the \textit{local validity of SR} (epistemological/operational equivalence). See  \cite{Lehmkuhl2022-LEHTEP} for details.}

Earman and Friedman wryly observed that “there are almost as many interpretations of this principle as there are authors of books on relativity theory” \citep[p.~329]{Earman1973}. 
 One of the most influential formulations—often attributed to \cite{pauli2013theory} and also referred to as the \textit{infinitesimal} (or \textit{local}) EP (see \citealp{Norton1985}), asserts that gravitational motion is \textit{locally} indistinguishable from inertial motion.
 It could also be put it in the following way (see \citealp[p.68]{weinberg1972gravitation}):
\begin{quote}
    \textit{\lq{}Locally\rq{}, the effects of a gravitational field are equivalent to those experienced in a non-inertial reference frame. Thus, gravity can be cancelled \lq{}locally\rq{} and this possibility defines \lq{}local\rq{} inertial frames where $\Gamma^a_{bc}=0$.}\footnote{I include scare quotes on the term \textit{local(ly)} since it is a term which will require unpacking. In more explicit terms, the local (Strong) EP can be formulated as \cite{hartle2003gravity} does: \lq{}\lq{} A concrete expression of this physical idea [i.e., of the equivalence principle] is the requirement that, given a metric \( g_{\mu\nu} \) in one system of coordinates, at each point \( P \) of spacetime it is possible to introduce new coordinates \( x'^{\mu} \) such that $g'_{\mu\nu} (x'_{P}) = \eta_{\mu\nu}$, where \( \eta_{\mu\nu} = \text{diag}(-1, 1, 1, 1) \) is the Minkowski metric of flat spacetime,and \( x'^{\mu}_P \) are the coordinates locating the point \( P \). This requirement is one of the assumptions of general relativity.\rq{}\rq{}}
\end{quote}
This principle, rooted in Einstein's original insights towards the construction of GR (his \lq{}happiest thought\rq{}), was initially proposed as a \textit{global} statement, but later reformulated to emphasise local equivalence.
This led to the \lq{}\textit{vulgata}\rq{} of the EP, according to which ‘\textit{gravity and fictitious, non-inertial forces are two sides of the same coin}’ and that the gravitational field \lq{}has only a relative existence\rq{}, since in a specific reference frame it can be cancelled, restoring inertiality of motion $\bigl( \frac{d^2x^a}{d\tau^2}=0 \bigr)$.

However, Einstein in the inception of the principle considered the case of a \textit{homogeneous} gravitational field produced by uniform, non-rotational acceleration in Minkowski spacetime, and not a generic gravitational field and generic acceleration.\footnote{As a historical note that I think is relevant and not emphasised enough, the first (rough) idea of the Einsteinian global version of the equivalence principle dates back to \cite{newton1687principia}'s \textit{Principia}. In Corollary VI, Newton introduces accelerated systems that can be treated, for practical purposes, as if they were at rest or in uniform motion (‘quasi-inertial’ systems). \begin{quote}
    \textbf{Corollary VI to the laws of motion}:
If bodies are moved in any way among themselves, and are urged
by equal accelerative forces along parallel lines, they will all
continue to move among themselves in the same way as if they
were not acted on by those forces (ivi, p. 20.)
\end{quote}}
Pauli subsequently generalised the argument, claiming that \textit{any} gravitational field can be nullified pointwise by a suitable diffeomorphism, in much the same way as fictitious forces in non‑inertial frames.
Thus, in a sense reversing Einstein's argument, the infinitesimal EP became the standard version adopted, spreading the belief that one can always transform away an arbitrary gravitational field in GR within an \textit{infinitesimal} region of spacetime and that it is possible to consider gravity to be equivalent to a non-inertial force and, as such, nullifiable pointwisely in the same way that the (components of the) Levi-Civita connections are nullifiable point by point. See \cite{Norton1985} for a thorough historical and philosophical analysis.


A closer examination reveals that the very notion of \lq{}local\rq{} warrants scrutiny, as it can be interpreted in two distinct ways:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Local at a point:} This refers to an infinitesimal neighbourhood around a single manifold point on the manifold. In the limit, this neighbourhood reduces to the point itself, which has Lebesgue measure zero in the ambient spacetime. In coordinate-based terminology, this notion corresponds to \textit{Riemann normal coordinate}s (adapted to a point).
\item \textbf{Local along a geodesic:} This pertains to an infinitesimal \lq{}tubular\rq{} neighbourhood surrounding a timelike geodesic. While the neighbourhood itself has Lebesgue measure zero as a subset of spacetime, the geodesic admits a well-defined affine parameterisation with a length (or proper time) that may be finite or unbounded. In coordinate-based terminology, this notion corresponds to \textit{Fermi normal coordinates} (adapted to a geodesic) \citep{fermi1922sopra}. 
\end{itemize}

As I shall argue in \S\ref{deviation}, in both interpretations the EP remains a \lq{}useful fantasy\rq{}.
Realistic physical systems are extended in space\footnote{For some authors, objects are also extended in time. See, for example, the case of four-dimensionalism \citep{Sider2001-SIDFDA,sep-temporal-parts}. Personally, I consider this view to be intrinsically flawed in GR, given the local nature of time and at most limited to special relativity.},  making strict pointwise or \lq{}tubular\rq{} localisation inapplicable.\footnote{This fact is the other side of the coin of \cite{Hilbert_1984}'s claim that “the infinite is nowhere to be found in reality, no matter what experiences, observations, and knowledge are appealed to.” Like infinity, the infinitely small is a mathematical concept, but not a physical one.} 

Thus, although \lq{}morally\rq{}, the standard story that special relativity is locally valid in GR is certainly correct, appealing to the local validity of special relativity or the pointwise vanishing of $\Gamma^{a}{}_{bc}$ is at best a \emph{useful fiction} when one seeks a definition of inertial motion for arbitrary extended matter.


%This critique of the concept of "locality" is useful to shed light on some criticalities of the strategy of \citeauthor{EinsteinGrommer} I put forth in \S \ref{TESTPARTICLE}, whose aim was to show the validity of the geodesic principle for arbitrarily large bodies.


I contend that a more robust approach demands questioning the ontological status of geodesics themselves, which are inherently infinitesimal—and thus singular—regions of spacetime.






\section{A Useful Fantasy: The Test Particle Approximation}\label{TESTPARTICLE}
\begin{quote}
\textit{If you wish to learn from the theoretical physicist anything about the methods which he uses, I would give you the following piece of advice: Don't listen to his words, examine his achievements. For to the discoverer in that field, the constructions of his imagination appear so necessary and so natural that he is apt to treat them not as the creations of his thoughts but as given realities.}

\rightline{\citealp{Einstein_1934}}
\end{quote}

%To recapitulate, in \S\eqref{sec1} I presented the common definitions of inertial motion and the principle of inertia in classical mechanics, constructed in terms of force-free motion, emphasising their circularity.
%In \S\eqref{geodesic}, on the other hand, I used the concept of geodesic motion to define \textit{local} inertial motion in GR, but again we encountered obstacles to the validity of this approach to a viable definition of inertia. In particular, I pointed out how the very notion of \textit{locality} clashes with the physical extension of all realistic objects from fundamental particles to planets.\footnote{Even an electron, often defined as a \lq{}point charge\rq{} thought of as structureless and without dimension, cannot actually be a point in the geometric sense of the term. If a charged particle were a true point, its charge would be concentrated at an infinitesimal location. According to classical electrodynamics, the electric field strength around a point charge increases without bound as you approach the charge. When you try to compute the energy stored in this field (by integrating the energy density over all space), the result diverges to infinity. An infinite self-energy is not just a mathematical inconvenience—it signals that the model is unphysical. Real physical objects must have finite energy, so a point charge with infinite self-energy is a sign that the assumption of a zero spatial extension is not viable. In quantum field theory (QFT), particles such as electrons are often modelled as point-like entities. However, the infinities that arise in such calculations require the process of renormalisation, where infinite quantities are systematically absorbed into redefined ("renormalised") parameters like mass and charge. While renormalisation allows predictions to match experiments remarkably well, the necessity of this procedure has led some physicists to speculate that the point-like description might only be an effective theory. It may be that a more fundamental description of nature reveals that particles have a finite size or an internal structure that regularizes these infinities.}

To recap, in \S\ref{sec1} I reviewed the standard definitions of inertial motion and the Principle of Inertia in classical mechanics—formulated in terms of force‑free motion—and highlighted their inherent circularity.  In \S\ref{geodesic} I then adopted the notion of geodesic motion to define \emph{local} inertial motion in GR, yet again encountered serious obstacles.  In particular, I noted that the very concept of \emph{locality} conflicts with the finite spatial extension of all realistic bodies, from electrons to planets.\footnote{Even an electron, though often idealised as a “point charge”, cannot truly be a geometric point: classical electrodynamics predicts an infinite self‑energy for a genuine point charge, rendering the model unphysical.  In quantum field theory, renormalisation is required to absorb such infinities, suggesting that the point‑like description may only be effective, with a more fundamental theory assigning particles a finite size or internal structure.}
In this section I examine additional difficulties that arise when one interprets geodesic motion as the criterion of local inertial motion for \emph{actual} bodies in GR.\footnote{Though I restrict attention to GR here, analogous remarks apply to Newton–Cartan theory (cf.\ fn.~\ref{NCT}).}


A geodesic typically describes the motion of a \textit{test particle}: an object so approximated that it does not perturb the spacetime metric.
So, the geodesic equation --- $\frac{Du^a}{D\tau}\equiv u^b\nabla_{b}u^a =0$, where $u^a$ is the four-velocity of the test particle--- provides the ‘‘law’’ for how gravitating bodies \textit{hover} the given metric field, without affecting it.
This approximation  provides a mathematically tractable approximation, but it diverges from physical reality: every body exerts its own gravitational influence, inducing \emph{backreaction} effects that generally spoil exact geodesic motion. As \cite{Anderson1967-en} observed:
\begin{quote}
    The motion of an otherwise free particle in a gravitational field will be governed by the geodesic equation [\dots] The gravitational field appearing in these equations is the \textit{total} gravitational field, which is influenced by the presence of the particle both directly through the contribution of its own field and through its effect on whatever other sources of gravitational fields are present. Because of the intrinsic nonlinearity of the Einstein equations, these two contributions are inextricably intertwined in the total field so that there is no simple way that we can eliminate self-interactions [\dots] Only for the
limiting case in which we can neglect the influence of the particle on the other
sources can we subtract off this self-interaction and take [the geodesic equation] to describe the motion of a free particle in a gravitational field (ivi, p.353).
\end{quote}

So, following \cite{mach1919}'s dictum,  the geodesic law of inertia is at best a \lq{}\lq{}\textit{tolerably accurate approximation}\rq{}\rq{} under specific conditions, but ultimately remains a theoretical construct \citep{thebault:2021b}.

The test-particle approximation, though a cornerstone of theoretical physics, represents a model that simplifies complex interactions.
This perspective highlights the broader philosophical implications of my exploration of the difficulties in defining a realistic concept of inertia, started in \S\ref{EQUIVALENCE} .
Embracing this challenge, I aim to reconcile the theoretical elegance of GR with the practical limitations imposed by real-world physical systems.


A particularly incisive critique of the geodesic principle in GR is provided by Tamir \citep{Tamir2012}.  He formulates a three‑part argument:
\begin{quote}
Specifically, I argue for the following three claims.
First, [\dots] massive bodies are [n]ever guaranteed to follow geodesic paths.
Second, [\dots] extended massive bodies generically deviate from uniformly geodesic paths.
[\dots] Third, thanks to certain mathematical theorems concerning distribution theory, alternative representations of massive bodies as unextended "point" particles must result either in precluding the possibility of coupling the particle to the spacetime metric in a way that is coherent with Einstein’s field equations or in having to excise the particle (and its would-be path) from spacetime entirely.
This three-pronged argument reveals that [\dots] the geodesic principle in such a way requires that either the gravitating body is not massive, its existence violates Einstein’s field equations, or it does not exist within the spacetime manifold at all (let alone along a geodesic) (ivi, p.137-138).
\end{quote}

\noindent In what follows, I concentrate on his first and third criticisms; the second will be addressed in \S\ref{deviation}.

Tamir’s analysis suggests that the geodesic principle applies only to \emph{unextended test particles}, which are manifestly highly approximated models of realistic bodies. 
Yet, almost contradictorily, in GR, unlike other classical theories, the geodesic equations are not logically independent from Einstein's equations (EFEs) which take into account the stress-energy content of matter.
How can it work for test particles? Did I not just state that \textit{only} test bodies follow geodesics and that, by definition, test bodies do not contribute to the dynamics of the metric, having no backreaction?

Let me untangle what is going on.
The derivability of the geodesic equation from the EFEs hints\footnote{See \cite{Weatherall2011} for a comparative study of the status of the geodesic \textit{theorem} in GR and in geometrised Newtonian gravitation. \cite{Weatherall2019} points out that the role of EFEs is not crucial to the theorem. The geodesic theorem is a theorem insofar as it can be derived under appropriate conditions from the geometric structures of the theory and the role they play in defining the matter dynamics, whatever that may be. From this observation the possibility of also defining a geodesic theorem for other relativistic theories besides GR or even for Newtonian theories arises. A different viewpoint, pointing to the \lq{}special nature\rq{} of GR in explaining geodesic motion, is notably found in \cite{Brown2005-kq}, but see also \cite{Sus2014} and \cite{Samaroo2018} who emphasise the dynamical nature of the metric in GR. \label{fngeodetictheorem}} at the possibility of defining the \lq{}geodesic principle\rq{} as a \lq{}geodesic \textit{theorem}\rq{}, rather than an independent principle.
In detail, the geodesic theorem in GR (also called \textit{Geroch-Jang Theorem}) runs as follows (see \citealp[p.22, Theorem 3.1]{Weatherall2016-WEAIME}; see also \citealp[p.146]{malament2012topics}):

\begin{thm}\label{GJ}
Let $(\mathcal{M},g_{ab})$ be a relativistic spacetime, and suppose $\mathcal{M}$ is oriented. Let $\gamma:I\rightarrow \mathcal{M}$ be a smooth imbedded curve.  Suppose that given any open subset $O$ of $\mathcal{M}$ containing $\gamma[I]$, there exists a smooth symmetric field $T^{ab}$ with the following properties.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \label{sdec} $T^{ab}$ satisfies the \emph{strengthened dominant energy condition}, i.e., given any timelike covector $\xi_a$ at any point in $\mathcal{M}$, $T^{ab}\xi_a\xi_b\geq 0$ and either $T^{ab}=\mathbf{0}$ or $T^{ab}\xi_a$ is timelike;
\item \label{cons}$T^{ab}$ satisfies the \emph{conservation condition}, i.e., $\nabla_a T^{ab}=\mathbf{0}$;
\item \label{inside}$\text{supp}({T^{ab})}\subset O$; and
\item \label{non-vanishing}there is at least one point in $O$ at which $T^{ab}\neq \mathbf{0}$.
\end{enumerate}
Then $\gamma$ is a timelike curve that can be reparametrized as a geodesic.
\end{thm}

To understand the validity of the theorem, recall that in GR the geodesic equation for some matter fields can be \textit{deduced} from the EFEs enforcing \(\nabla_a T^{ab} = 0.\)
This is a brute geometrical consequence of the\textit{ Bianchi's identities} $\nabla_aG^{ab}=0$.
So, in the course of the proof we \textit{do} consider the stress–energy tensor of the matter that obeys the geodesic equation.\footnote{This means first knowing the form of the tensor $T^{ab}$. As can be easily guessed, even this is strictly an illicit move as the stress-energy tensor needs the metric to be defined, and viceversa!
In this fact lies the non-linearity of EFEs and the fact that the equations of motion are not independent of the field equations. Caveats stated in fn.\ref{fngeodetictheorem} applies.}


I point out some perplexities concerning this inference guided by the Bianchi's identities.
The first is that, strictly speaking, this inference concerns only matter that appears as a source in EFEs and not \textit{test} --- as opposed to sourcing ---- matter.
Secondly, as stated in \citealp[p.22]{Weatherall2016-WEAIME}: \lq{}\lq{}a principal difficulty in trying to derive the geodesic principle as a theorem [that is, as a consequence of dynamics and not as an independent postulate] concerns a kind of ontological mismatch between the geodesic principle and the rest of general relativity: namely, general relativity is a field theory, whereas the geodesic principle is a statement concerning point particles.\rq{}\rq{} (my aside).

The usual response is to regard test particles as “small bits” of extended matter and to show that, under suitable approximations, these “bits” follow geodesics.  Yet this, too, is merely \textit{a useful fantasy}.

%Moreover, note that timelike (that is,\textit{ sub-luminal}) geodesic motion for matter is not directly given by the geometric structure of GR. What I mean is that it is not the case that for \textit{any} stress-energy tensor associated with matter, that matter will follow a geodesic motion. As Theorem \eqref{GJ} shows, it is necessary to introduce an additional assumption: the \emph{strengthened dominant energy condition} \eqref{sdec}. Only in this way do we obtain \textit{timelike} geodesics. To make precise what I said a moment ago, it is not true \textit{in general} that the conservation law $\nabla_a T^{ab} = 0$  automatically imply that the fluid elements follow geodesic motion; pressure gradients or other interactions can lead to non‐geodesic accelerations.

Moreover, note that timelike (that is,\textit{ sub-luminal}) geodesic motion for matter is not directly given by the geometric structure of GR.
What I mean is that theorem  \eqref{GJ} requires the strengthened dominant energy condition to guarantee \emph{timelike} geodesics.  Absent this, or in the presence of pressure gradients or internal stresses, \(\nabla_aT^{ab}=0\) does not imply geodesic motion.

However, in the special case of a perfectly homogeneous, pressureless (dust) fluid—often used as an idealisation for non-interacting matter—the absence of pressure ensures that no forces other than gravity act on the freely falling fluid elements. As a result, each element follows a geodesic, despite its backreaction contribution being non-zero.\footnote{The pressureless dust model represents a continuous, uniform distribution of matter rather than a realistic extended body. In reality, every body described by rigorous physical theories possesses angular momentum distributions and internal properties (e.g., internal stresses or electromagnetic charges) that lead to deviations from geodesic motion. }

An example of such a special case is the \textit{FLRW cosmic fluid}, where non-relativistic matter is modelled as pressureless dust.\footnote{It is important to note that the FLRW framework is not limited to a single “dust” component, but it allows the total cosmic fluid to be a mixture of several components, each with its own equation of state. These include non-relativistic matter (e.g., cold dark matter or baryonic matter), radiation, and dark energy.
In the FLRW model, the geodesic motion of fluid elements applies only to pressureless dust (non-relativistic matter). Relativistic components like radiation or dark energy exhibit pressure/stress terms in their energy-momentum tensors, causing deviations from geodesic motion of the fluid. Individual photons, however, always follow null geodesics in GR, independent of the fluid description.}
For the cosmic fluid, the energy–momentum tensor is given by
\begin{equation}
T^{ab} = \rho\, u^a u^b,
\end{equation}
where \(\rho>0\) is the energy density and \(u^a\) is the four-velocity field of the fluid.
The absence of pressure terms ensures that:
\begin{equation}
\nabla_a T^{ab} = 0 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad u^b \nabla_b u^a = 0,
\end{equation}
which is precisely the geodesic equation.\footnote{Conversely, assuming geodesic motion for the dust fluid implies that $T^{ab}$ is divergence-free and satisfy the strengthened dominant energy condition \eqref{sdec} \citep{Weatherall2019}. In even slightly more general cases, such as a perfect fluid but not a dust, the Euler equation takes the form $$u^b\nabla_bu^a=-\cfrac{1}{\rho+P}(g^{ab}+u^au^b)\nabla_bP,$$
where $\nabla_bP$ represents the pressure gradient. In the most general case of an imperfect, charged fluid we have other contribution like bulk viscosity, shear viscosity, heat flux, electromagnetic forces etc. on the right-hand side, that are are what cause the fluid elements to deviate from geodesic motion.}


\cite{Tamir2012} reports that \cite{EinsteinGrommer} rejected this way of modelling matter because Einstein in particular had a strong distrust of the representation of matter by continuous fields, seeing it as a '\textit{low-grade}' description \citep{einstein1954ideas} that did not do justice to the known properties of matter, in particular its particle nature.\footnote{The debate concerning the fundamental ontology—whether it is based on particles or fields—remains a significant topic of discussion, particularly within the foundations of Quantum Field Theory (QFT) \citep{kuhlmann2002ontological, Glick2016, Jia2022}.} Furthermore, as seen above in the cosmological case, derivations that assume geodesic motion for extended bodies often require significant symmetry assumptions.


Einstein and Grommer propose a way (which they called the \lq{}\textit{third way}\rq{} to address matter-field dualism) that avoids both the continuous representation of matter-energy and the use of unrealistic point-like test particles, by \lq{}hiding\rq{} all stress-energy of matter along ‘\textit{singular worldlines}’ and thus using only the EFEs in vacuum ($R_{ab} = 0$).
According to this strategy, geodesics represent singular trajectories in vacuum spacetime, where the stress-energy content of bodies is \lq{}\textit{compressed}\rq{} infinitesimally along each geodesic and \textit{hidden} from spacetime.
In this way, they reconciled the fact that the geodesic equation is not independent on the EFEs (as per the geodesic theorem) with the claim that backreacting bodies likewise follows geodesic motion.
In particular, by adopting this strategy, it is possible to construct a formal proof to derive geodesic motion even for bodies with significant mass and not only for test particles.
This kind of proof is commonly referred to as the \textit{singularity proof}.
In more detail, the procedure is as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item One firstly excises the singular curves from the spacetime manifold.
\item Once excised, these curves no longer contribute to the source terms in the EFEs. The bodies are then \lq{}confined\rq{} to these curves, placing them "outside" the spacetime manifold and thus eliminating their role as sources in the EFEs, becoming for all practical purposes as \textit{test} bodies. However, differently from test bodies, the stress-energy content is not ‘thrown away’ \textit{a priori}, but managed in such a way that it is not taken into account (a similar move is adopted in \citealp{Ehlers2004}).
\item These excised curves and bodies are then reintroduced and they align with the geodesics of the vacuum solutions in the reconstructed spacetime.
\end{itemize}

As stated in \cite{Tamir2012}, the view of a geodesic as a \lq{}singular curve\rq{} describing the motion of a \lq{}compressed\rq{} \textit{actual} body presents numerous problems (see also \citealp{Weatherall2020}).\footnote{The criticisms of the concept of geodesic motion for arbitrarily extended bodies is not limited only to the singularity proof. In \cite{Tamir2012}, several proofs to derive a valid geodesic motion for non-test bodies are analysed, i.e. for objects a non-zero stress-energy contribution to the EFEs. However, all these proofs are shown to fail. Furthermore, matter field solutions for various hyperbolic systems, such as Maxwell's, Klein-Gordon, and Dirac equations, cannot conform to geodesic motion constraints since wave equations typically lack solutions confined to small, spatially compact curve neighbourhoods. \citep{Geroch2018}.}
For example, in a singular region the stress-energy density associated with the body becomes infinite, with the result that it would no longer be possible to define a spatiotemporal path, as the metric becomes undefined. Furthermore, \textit{qua} singular, the supposed path of such geodesic is not even definable in the spacetime manifold.\footnote{This situation is very similar to the definition of the singularity of a black hole. See \cite{Curiel2019-CURSAB-2}. See also \cite[p.12]{EarmanJohn1995Bcwa}.}
Finally, every the proof based on stress-energy distributions compressed on worldlines come up against the theorem of \cite{Geroch1987}, which shows that there are no reasonable solutions of the EFEs in which the source is concentrated on a one-dimensional curve. 
Thus, the geodesic principle not only does not derive logically from the EFEs, but an evolution that is precisely geodesic would typically contravene the EFEs in cases involving non-negligible massive bodies.


From the discussion conducted, it can therefore be concluded that  the geodesic principle (or theorem) applies \emph{at most} to unextended test particles.\footnote{In light of the link between EFEs and geodesic equation, the adoption of the \textit{test particle approximation}, which renders the dynamics of test bodies \textit{independent} from the EFEs, could implies two different approximations.
Firstly, as stressed above for the \lq{}third way\rq{} it could not \textit{necessarily} mean to \lq{}throw away and forget\rq{} the stress-energy content of the test body, but only to consider it negligible for the dynamics of interest.
This may also amount to consider some \textit{auxiliary} EFEs, containing only the stress-energy content of the test particle and then derive the corresponding geodesic motion compatible with the auxiliary metric. In such a way the "test" nature of the body is maintained with respect to the "principal" metric. This proposal is inspired by the definition of Auxiliary Reference Frames in \cite{BamontiGomes2024}. It also resonates with the \lq{}\cite{Infeld1949} approach\rq{} to restrict the Einsteinian geodesic principle to a specific kind of test particles whose mass is non-zero, but still can be ignored in the limit where the mass $m$ tends to zero ($m \rightarrow 0$).
As a second possibility, the adoption of the test particle approximation could mean to consider the stress energy content of the test matter as negligible. \textit{Sic et simpliciter}. This latter case aligns with the discussion in \cite{Bamonti2023}, where test bodies are used as reference frames whose stress-energy content is neglected, referred to as \lq{}Dynamical Reference Frames\rq{}. Of course, being an approximation is not how real bodies behave.}
Since every realistic object has finite spatial extension—including those approximated as “test” bodies—the notion of geodesics is best regarded as a \emph{useful fantasy}, rather than a true characterisation of inertial motion.

Accordingly, in the next section I shall argue that if the locally inertial motion is defined as geodesic motion (that is, the motion along a geodesic in a Fermi normal inertial frame), then the geodesic, inertial motion cannot be the \textit{natural} motion of  any realistic, \textit{even approximated} (in the sense of a test body approximation), matter.




\section{Natural Motion is Not Inertial Motion: The Geodesic Deviation}\label{deviation}


The journey thus far has highlighted the difficulties in defining inertial motion from both classical and relativistic perspectives—ultimately revealing the role of geodesics in the real world as, at best, a \emph{useful fantasy}.  What remains?  How can we meaningfully speak of inertial motion for realistic material bodies?  At the terminus of our main road, a side lane emerges that separates \emph{natural motion} from \emph{inertial motion}.  Since Aristotle profound distinction between natural and forced motion, these notions have been treated as two sides of the same coin—natural motion being inertial, and vice versa—but I propose that they instead be distinguished.

In this section I argue that the quest to define the “inertial motion of real bodies” is misconceived.  If one begins with an erroneous question, one should not be surprised to find no satisfactory answer.  What does make sense is to speak, on the one hand, of the \emph{natural motion of real bodies}, and on the other, of the \emph{inertial motion of fictitious bodies}.

If—in line with \S\ref{geodesic} and \S\ref{TESTPARTICLE}—one identifies inertial motion with the local geodesic motion of test particles, one must then ask: what is the \emph{natural} motion of bodies that are both extended and backreacting?  The side lane leads to the notion of \emph{geodesic deviation motion}.

I now introduce a crucial working hypothesis.  For the present paper, I restrict attention to bodies that are \emph{extended} but \emph{non‑backreacting}, which I term \emph{quasi‑realistic bodies}.  The goal is to define a notion of natural motion solely for these bodies; the more complicated case of genuine backreaction will be addressed in more details in subsequent work.  As in any long voyage, pausing at intermediate waypoints clarifies the path forward.

Accordingly, the remainder of this section is organised as follows:

In \S\ref{5.1} I introduce the standard geodesic deviation formalism, which comprehensively formalises the dynamics of extended but not backreacting bodies: what are called here \lq{}quasi-realistic bodies\rq{}.

In \S\ref{5.2} I sketch how this formalism might be modified to include backreaction effects—thus addressing the true case of natural motion, which however will be analysed in more details elsewhere.

In \S\ref{5.3} I present a cosmological example that illustrates and supports the distinction between inertial and natural motion.



%geodesic deviation

\subsection{Extended \textit{Test} Body: Tidal Effects}\label{5.1}
In GR, the geodesic deviation formalises how nearby geodesics diverge or converge in curved spacetime.

\begin{comment}
Consider a one-parameter family (often named a \textit{congruence}) of geodesics \( x^\mu(\tau, s) \), where \( \tau \) is proper time along each geodesic and \( s \) labels neighbouring geodesics. The \textit{deviation vector field} \( \xi^\mu = \frac{\partial x^\mu}{\partial s} \), connecting infinitesimally separated geodesics with tangent vector \( u^\mu = \frac{dx^\mu}{d\tau} \), evolves via the second covariant derivative:
\begin{equation}
\frac{D^2 \xi^\mu}{D\tau^2} = -R^\mu_{\; \nu \rho \sigma} u^\nu \xi^\rho u^\sigma,
\end{equation}
where $\frac{D}{D\tau}=u^\mu\nabla_\mu$ and \( R^\mu_{\; \nu \rho \sigma} \) is the Riemann curvature tensor. This equation—mathematically equivalent to the Jacobi equation governing Jacobi fields in differential geometry \citep{Wilkins_Riemannian}—describes \textit{relative acceleration} between geodesics and applies to\textit{ each pair} of neighbouring geodesics, with $\xi^\mu$ varying across the congruence.
\end{comment}

Consider a smooth map
\begin{equation}
\gamma: I \times J \to \mathcal{M},
\end{equation}
where \(\mathcal{M}\) is a differentiable manifold, \(I\) is an interval parametrising proper time $\tau$ along geodesics, and \(J\) labels the different geodesics in a one-parameter \textit{congruence} of geodesics. For each fixed \(s \in J\), the curve  $\gamma_s(\tau) = \gamma(\tau, s)$ is a geodesic in \(\mathcal{M}\) and assigns to each pair $(\tau,s)$ a point in $\mathcal{M}$.\footnote{Some texts choose to denote the image of  \( \gamma \) as  \( x^a(\tau,s) \) where the superscript \( a \) does not denote a vector in the sense of $x^a$ being an element of a tangent space. Rather,  \( x^a(\tau,s) \) represents the abstract "position" of a point on the manifold \( \mathcal{M} \).}

Along this congruence one defines two fundamental vector fields:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The \textbf{tangent vector field} 
    \begin{equation}
    u^a = \left(\frac{\partial \gamma}{\partial \tau}\right)^a,
    \end{equation}
    which represents the velocity field along each geodesic.\footnote{The derivative $\frac{\partial \gamma}{\partial \tau}$
is the \textit{pushforward} $d\gamma(\frac{\partial}{\partial\tau})$ induced by $\gamma$ on the basis vector \(\frac{\partial}{\partial \tau}\) from the parameter space  \( I \times J \) to the  tangent space  \( T_{\gamma(\tau,s)}\mathcal{M} \) at the point  \( \gamma(\tau,s)=x^a(\tau,s) \).}
    \item The \textbf{deviation vector field} is 
    \begin{equation}
    \xi^a = \left(\frac{\partial \gamma}{\partial s}\right)^a,
    \end{equation}
    which connects points on infinitesimally nearby geodesics.
\end{itemize}

The evolution of the deviation field is given by the geodesic deviation (or Jacobi) equation \citep{Wilkins_Riemannian}. This equation describes how the \textit{relative acceleration} of nearby geodesics is influenced by curvature:
\begin{equation}
\nabla_u \nabla_u \xi = - R^a{}_{bcd}\, u^b\, \xi^c\, u^d,\label{geodesicdeveq}
\end{equation}
where \(\nabla_u:=u^a\nabla_a\) denotes the covariant derivative along the vector field \(u^a\) and \(R^a_{bcd}\) is the Riemann curvature tensor acting on the pair \((u^a,\xi^a)\).\footnote{In the usual coordinate-dependent formulation, one typically writes the geodesics as \(x^\mu(\tau,s)\), with
$u^\mu = \frac{dx^\mu}{d\tau}$ and $\quad \xi^\mu = \frac{\partial x^\mu}{\partial s}.$
The same geodesic deviation equation is then expressed as 
\[
\frac{D^2 \xi^\mu}{D\tau^2} = -R^\mu_{\; \nu\rho\sigma}\, u^\nu\, \xi^\rho\, u^\sigma,
\]
with \(\frac{D}{D\tau} = u^\mu \nabla_\mu\).}


Each freely falling geodesic naturally defines a \textit{local inertial frame}—often constructed using Fermi normal coordinates. This frame serves as the "yardstick" against which you measure.
While individual geodesic experience no proper acceleration (as describing free-falling objects), spacetime curvature induces tidal forces that differentially accelerate their separation \( \xi^a \).\footnote{As already stressed in \S\ref{EQUIVALENCE}, the EP states that it is always possible to select a locally inertial frame in which the Levi-Civita connections vanish, and the metric takes the form of that in flat spacetime. Conversely, in a flat spacetime, one can introduce a reference frame that locally mimics the presence of an arbitrary gravitational field. Within such a frame, the \textit{apparent} gravitational force can be inferred by analysing the motion of a single particle. However, distinguishing whether this force is genuine or merely an artifact of the frame choice requires examining the \textit{relative motion} of nearby particles—specifically, by analysing the behaviour of neighbouring geodesics.}

If two freely falling point-like test particles carry (massless and infinitesimal, idealised) accelerometers, their accelerometers would read \textit{zero} individually (they are in free fall).
However, each of them would measure a (relative) acceleration caused by spacetime curvature encoded by the Riemann tensor.
Crucially, the relative acceleration (expressed here in some coordinates for ease of understanding) $A^\mu:=\frac{D^2 \xi^\mu}{D\tau^2}$ is \lq{}intrinsic\rq{} to spacetime curvature and it is measured \textit{by comparing the motion of the geodesics of the couple with respect to the local inertial frame established along one of them}.
Relative acceleration is intrinsic in the sense that (per equation \eqref{geodesicdeveq}) reflects only the intrinsic curvature. Intrinsic curvature in GR is captured by the Riemann tensor and refers to the curvature that is determined entirely by measurements made within the manifold itself—without any reference to how the manifold might be embedded in a higher-dimensional space.
The Riemann tensor that cannot be cancelled at a point or along a geodesic by choosing an appropriate reference frame, thus the effects of intrinsic curvature in terms of the relative acceleration of locally inertial systems along geodesics are also present in a locally inertial reference frame.
Thus, the \textit{relative} acceleration refers to how one geodesic appears to accelerate with respect to the other.
In other words, if one attaches a local inertial frame to one of the geodesics, the second covariant derivative of the separation vector gives the acceleration of the second geodesic relative to that first one.



The geodesic deviation formalism underpins our understanding of tidal effects in gravitational fields, as it links local geometric properties of spacetime with observable phenomena such as the stretching and squeezing experienced by extended test bodies in a gravitational field: for example the gravitational wave strain detected by interferometers like LIGO \citep{LIGO} (a passing wave alternately stretches and compresses distances perpendicular to its propagation direction, directly measurable as relative acceleration).

For extended \textit{non-rigid} bodies (that is, with some internal structure), modelled as a congruence of \textit{timelike, non-intersecting} geodesics, the geodesic deviation formalism describes \textit{dynamically} how spacetime curvature dynamically distorts the shape and volume of the congruence (that is, of the body) \cite[\S6.1]{landsman2021}.\footnote{In relativity, the concept of a rigid body is fundamentally unviable because any instantaneous transmission of force across an object would require signals to travel faster than light. Thus, special relativistic frameworks often invoke the concept of \textit{Born rigidity}, a much more restrictive condition that only applies to very specific motions and which requires that internal structure of an object remains unchanged under motion in its own frame. In GR, Born rigidity becomes even more problematic to maintain due to tidal effects. For discussions see \cite{Giulini2006,Ziyang}.}

Using a perturbative approach, one can also describe the extended structure of the body using a \textit{multipole expansion} of its stress-energy tensor. This is much like how we describe extended charge distributions in electrodynamics. The lowest order (monopole) gives the total mass-energy of the body; the next order (dipole) does not contribute to gravitational radiation since I am assuming \textit{zero spin}; the quadrupole is the first non-trivial term capturing internal structure and dynamics of the body. Of course, in this context, the stress-energy tensor is not a source of curvature, but a tool to define the body’s internal structure.

Alternatively, at the \lq{}collective level\rq{} of the whole extended body, the effects of the geodesic deviation are encoded by the \lq{}\textit{quadrupole tidal force} \rq{} $F^a_{\text{tidal}}\propto-\nabla^aR_{bcde}Q^{bcde}$, where $Q^{abcd}$ is  the \textit{quadrupole tensor}, antisymmetric in first two and last two indices (see \citealp[\S6]{dixon1970dynamics}).
The quadrupole encodes the body’s \textit{resistance} to tidal forces (stretching/squeezing) caused by spacetime curvature and describes how stress-energy is distributed inside the extended body relative to its centre of mass. In particular the collective dynamics is governed by a \textit{generalised} Mathisson-Papapetrou-Dixon formalism, truncated at quadrupole order. \textit{Assuming zero spin}, the equation of motion for (the centre of mass of) the extended body is: $ \frac{Dp^a}{D\tau}=F^a_{\text{tidal}}$ (see fn.\ref{fnMDP}). It is therefore easy to see that the extended body does not follow a geodesic equation $\frac{Dp^a}{D\tau}=0$. 

To clarify, at the dynamical level, the geodesic deviation equation specifies the \textit{source} of tidal forces (Riemann curvature) acting \textit{within} the body, while the quadrupole force encodes how the body’s \textit{internal structure} (via $Q^{abcd}$) modifies its \textit{overall motion} in response to tidal forces. Thus, in a sense, both descriptions are part of the same picture — but they describe different aspects: internal vs. external. 


The timelike four-velocity field $u^a$ defines the following tensors of some importance for studying \textit{kinematically} the collective motion of the congruence of geodesics: the \textit{expansion scalar} ($\theta$ ) measuring how the cross-sectional area of the congruence changes; the \textit{shear tensor} (\( \sigma_{ab} \)) describing how the shape of the congruence is distorted; and the \textit{vorticity tensor} (\( \omega_{ab} \)) which accounts for the local rotation of the geodesics around one another.\footnote{Vorticity alone does not guarantee rigid rotation. Only when expansion and shear are absent does vorticity correspond to a (Born) rigid, distance-preserving twist.} 
These quantities are related as follows:
\begin{equation}
\nabla_a u_b = \omega_{ab} + \sigma_{ab} + \frac{1}{3} \theta h_{ab},\label{kinematic}
\end{equation}
where $h_{ab}=g_{ab}+u_a u_b$ is the \textit{projection tensor} that projects tensors onto the 3D spatial hypersurfaces orthogonal to $u^a$ and $\nabla_a u_b=k_{ab}$ is also called the \textit{extrinsic curvature}.\footnote{For example, the \textit{Raychaudhuri equation} governs \( \theta \) :
\[
\frac{d\theta}{d\tau} = -\frac{1}{3}\theta^2 - \sigma^{ab}\sigma_{ab} + \omega^{ab}\omega_{ab} - R_{ab} u^a u^b,
\]
linking expansion to the Ricci tensor \( R_{ab} \). On the other hand, shear and vorticity couple to the Weyl tensor \( C_{abcd} \), encoding tidal forces. In a sense, the kinematic decomposition of  equation \eqref{kinematic} provides the initial “state” of the congruence, while the geodesic deviation (and the Raychaudhuri equation) provides the dynamics—how that state evolves due to both the intrinsic deformation of the congruence and the curvature of spacetime.} 
 Equation \eqref{kinematic} captures the instantaneous “kinematic state” of the body, while \eqref{geodesicdeveq} govern its subsequent evolution under the influence of curvature.

Applications of this formalism include also e.g. FLRW cosmological expansion; radial shear near Schwartzschild black holes; and oscillatory shear from gravitational waves.\footnote{In particular, for FRLW model we have $\omega_{ab}=\sigma_{ab}=0$ and \( \theta \propto H \), where $H$ is the Hubble parameter \citep{weinberg1972gravitation}. This is due to the assumptions of homogeneity and isotropy. Furthermore, in this case, we set a preferred reference frame named the \textit{synchronous frame} in which $u^\mu=(1,0,0,0)$ and $g_{00}=1,g_{0i}=0$. Consequently, the projection operator coincide with the adapted 3-metric $h_{ij}$ to the 3D spatial hypersurfaces of global cosmic time. Deviations from FLRW can reintroduce shear and vorticity, but for \textit{hypersurface orthogonal congruence of timelike geodesics} satisfying $u_{[a}\nabla_b u_{c]}=0$ , typical of globally hyperbolic spacetimes, the \textit{vorticity} $\omega_{ab}$ is always zero.}

%---------------------------------------------------------------------


This refined picture invites precise definitions for (quasi‑)realistic bodies in free fall:

\begin{definition}
    \textbf{Inertial Motion. v.3}:  \label{INERTIA3} It is the \textit{approximated} motion of each of the infinite timelike geodesics that make up a congruence of geodesics composing a real object in free fall. It characterises the \textit{local} motion \textit{along} each geodesic of test, point-like constituents of the real object.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
    \textbf{Natural Motion}:  It is the \textit{relative motion} between distinct geodesics within a congruence of geodesics that make up a free-falling real object. Specifically, Natural Motion is defined by \textit{geodesic deviation} (plus self-interaction terms taking into account the backreaction).\footnote{An interesting line of research  is to show that the\lq{}(generalised) geodesic deviation motion\rq{} of a body is compatible with a ‘\textit{collective motion of individual parts}’ that follow geodesics clustering around a single timelike geodesic, functioning as an idealised \lq{}anchor model\rq{}. In this way, bodies can be expected to stick ‘close to’ a geodesic path for suitable spatial and temporal scales. Interestingly enough, this type of near-geodesic clustering is similar to that of a \textit{non-thermal universality phenomenon} \citep{Batterman2002-BATTDI}. An analysis in this direction was made in \cite{Tamir2013}.}

\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
    \textbf{(Local) INRF. v.3} It is an \textit{approximated, local} frame defined along \textit{each} geodesic in the congruence of geodesics that makes up a real body in free-fall. (The definition is the same as Definition \eqref{INRF2}, the difference being that it emphasises the approximated, unrealistic nature of a local INRF). \label{INRF3}

\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
    \textbf{PIN v.6}: Relative to a local \textit{inertial} reference frame $\{x^I\}$ (as defined in \ref{INRF3}), a body maintains its \textit{natural} motion defined by $\frac{D^2 \xi^I}{D\tau^2} = -R^I_{\; JKL}\, u^J\, \xi^K\, u^L\;+\;\text{(backreaction terms)}$ . \label{PIN6}
\end{definition}


In sum: a local inertial reference frame is defined along a chosen geodesic in a congruence and relative to this frame, the relative acceleration of a neighbouring geodesic (within the same congruence composing an extended body in free-fall) is computed using the (generalised) geodesic deviation equation

Note the emphasised words in Definition \ref{PIN6}: \lq{}\textit{inertial} \rq{} and \lq{}\textit{natural}\rq{}. 
Distinguishing between these two concepts removes the triviality and circularity of all the previous definitions of inertia, \textit{since the gravitational motion of realistic bodies does not coincide with the inertial geodesic motion.}

When a realistic object is in free fall, it does \textit{not} follow a geodesic inertial path, since the very notion of geodesics is only a useful fantasy, as it rests on a physically unrealisable notion of ‘locality’ (additional to non-backreaction approximations).

The argument of the \textit{inequivalence} of natural and inertial motion also reinforces the claim made in \S\ref{EQUIVALENCE}  that the EP, while invaluable as an operational guide, cannot be an exact physical principle concerning real physical systems.

A gravitational field is always characterised by a non-zero Riemann curvature tensor, which quantifies \textit{geodesic deviation}. 
The Riemann tensor cannot be nullified, \textit{not even locally}, thus second --- or higher --- order dynamical equations (e.g., wave equations for fields) may retain curvature-dependent terms (e.g., Riemann tensor components), \textit{even in local inertial frames} \citep{Brown2016}. This is also one of the arguments against the mantra of the ‘local validity of special relativity’ that I mentioned in footnote \eqref{fnlocalsr}.
The only way to eliminate tidal effects is to consider a sufficiently small spatio-temporal surround such that two geodesics are arbitrarily close. Thus, the geodesic deviation vector $\xi^a$, which appears in the second member of the equation \eqref{geodesicdeveq} and whose modulus $|\xi^a|$ represents the orthogonal spatiotemporal distance between two geodesics, is characterised by arbitrarily small components in a chosen frame $\xi^I<<\mathcal{O}(1)$. Therefore, even if in space-time the Riemann tensor is non-zero and has a finite value one can make the four-acceleration due to curvature arbitrarily small 
\begin{equation}
\frac{D^2 \xi^I}{D\tau^2} = -R^I_{\; JKL}\, u^J\, \xi^K\, u^L <<\mathcal{O}(1)
\end{equation}
Only in the limit of $\xi^I \to 0$ one can eliminate tidal effects also in some local sense.\footnote{What is the physical interpretation of this limit? In my opinion, it is like collapsing all of spacetime into a single 1-dimensional geodesic.}


That said, no measurement is \lq{}local\rq{} in the mathematical sense of the term expressed in \S\ref{EQUIVALENCE}, since experimental apparata always occupy some extended region of spacetime.
Therefore, local EP can only serve as a useful approximation dependent on the sensitivity of measurements, so it can be at most an \lq{}operational principle\rq{}.\footnote{There is an interesting connection between what I define \lq{}operational principle\rq{} and what \cite{Fletcher} calls \lq{}representational principle\rq{}. See also how \cite{bamontithebault} define the clock hypothesis as something like a \lq{}coordinative definition\rq{} in the sense of \cite{reichenbach:1928}, or also \cite{adlam:2022} who express themselves along the same \lq{}operational\rq{} lines.}
Thus, the EP holds \textit{approximately} in regions where tidal effects (spacetime curvature) are negligible \textit{relative to experimental precision}. \lq{}\lq{}If one's experimental apparata are insensitive to such effects, then
the [equivalence] principle may be regarded as holding approximately [locally]\rq{}\rq{} \citep{Read2018}.
Stating otherwise, we always have a finite $|\xi^a|$ and if the experimental precision is not fine enough and exceeds this amount, the tidal effects are negligible.
It follows immediately that the same reasoning applies to the validity of geodesic motion: since observational precision is inevitably limited and $|\xi^a|$ is less than the level of experimental precision or attention, a freely falling body is said to follow a geodesic.

Note that my approach can provide an immediate understanding, via the well-known geodesic deviation equation, for \cite{Ehlers2004}'s \lq{}epsilon-delta relation\rq{}, which quantifies (by a parameter $\epsilon$) how nearly-geodesic a curve followed by a body is and (by a parameter $\delta$) the limit on the gravitational effects of the body's backreaction (see also \citealp{Gralla2008}). In summary, $\epsilon$ serves as a measure of deviation or proximity from ideal geodesic behaviour. The smaller $\epsilon$ is, the closer the motion of the body is to following perfect geodesics. Thus, it plays the same role as the deviation vector $\xi^a$. The $\delta$ parameter, on the other hand, is linked to what I will call \lq{}self-force effects\rq{} in \S\ref{5.2}.



%outro






%This supports the rationale according to which the gravitational natural motion and the non-inertial motion (locally eliminable) are two separate concepts (see also \citealp{Brown2016}'s \lq{}Misconception 3\rq{}). While \textit{geodesic motion} can mimic non-inertial motion locally (via the Christoffels $\Gamma^a_{bc}$), gravitational natural motion and non-inertial motion are distinct, not only globally but \textit{even locally}.  


\subsection{A Sketch For Extended and \textit{Backreacting} Body: Self-Force Effects}\label{5.2}


When one considers an extended, spinless body whose gravitational backreaction on the surrounding spacetime is non‑negligible, the simple picture provided by the standard geodesic deviation must be refined.  The trajectory of such a body is governed not only by its internal multipolar structure—arising from its finite size—but also by the way its own stress–energy perturbs the geometry through which it moves.

In particular, two key complications arise which are known as \textit{Self-Force effects}. These effects, often relevant in the context of \textit{extreme mass-ratio inspirals} \citep{Barack2009,AmaroSeoane2018}, introduce additional contributions to the geodesic deviation equation, arising from:
\begin{description}
\item[\textit{Metric perturbations}]: the body’s own gravitational field induces a perturbation \(h_{ab}\) on an assumed background metric \(g^0_{ab}\).
\item[\textit{Tail effects}] : the gravitational field propagates in a curved spacetime in a non-local manner (the introduction of these effects is due to the work of \citealp{DeWitt1960}).
\end{description}


Once self-force effects are included, the relative acceleration between nearby geodesics receives extra contributions that depend on the body’s own gravitational field propagation.

These phenomena are treated perturbatively within the \emph{MiSaTaQuWa} formalism after Mino, Sasaki, Tanaka, Quinn, and Wald \citep{Mino1997,quinn1997axiomatic}.  The spacetime metric is split into a background and a perturbation sourced by the body’s stress-energy tensor:
\begin{equation}
    g_{ab}=g^{0}_{ab}+h_{ab}.
\end{equation}
This perturbative formalism assumes $h_{ab}<<g^0_{ab}$ and truncates multipole expansions at quadrupole order, avoiding the intractable non-linearities of the full EFEs. In fact, an exact approach is mathematically infeasible for general matter distributions and is only tractable under strong symmetry assumptions or in special cases.

Within this framework, the collective dynamics of the body are governed by a generalised Mathisson–Papapetrou–Dixon system, incorporating both:
\begin{equation}
  F^a_{\mathrm{tidal}}
  \;\propto\;
  -\,\nabla^aR_{bcde}\;Q^{bcde},
  \quad
  F^a_{\mathrm{self}}
  \;\propto\;
  -\,\nabla^a h^{\mathrm{tail,R}}_{ab},
\end{equation}
where \(Q^{bcde}\) is the quadrupole tensor of the body’s stress–energy distribution, and \(h^{\mathrm{tail,R}}_{ab}\) is the regularised tail part of the metric perturbation, obtained via a Green’s function approach that removes divergences \citep{dixon1970dynamics,Poisson2004,Pound2020,Mathews2022}.\footnote{This formalism underpins gravitational–wave modelling for missions such as \href{https://lisa.nasa.gov}{LISA} gravitational-wave modelling, in which tail effects produce measurable phase shifts in the observed waveform.}

Crucially, the self–force depends on these \emph{non‑local tail terms}, which rely on the body’s entire past history. 
A wave emitted by the body at some earlier time (given a foliation) can get scattered, then act back on the body at a later time.
This \lq{}echo\rq{} of the body's past gravitational activity, returning to influence it, is called the \lq{}\textit{tail effect}\rq{}.
These terms arise because in curved spacetime the retarded Green’s function has support \textit{inside} the light cone, allowing the body to \lq{}feel\rq{} the influence of its past gravitational field \citep{Caldwell1993}.
In other words, unlike flat spacetime, where interactions propagate strictly \textit{on} the light cone, in curved spacetime waves can also propagate \textit{inside} the light cone, giving rise to genuinely non‑local dynamics.
Think of it like a body "feeling its own field" after it has scattered off the background.

Correspondingly, the evolution of the deviation vector in equation \eqref{geodesicdeveq} is modified, yielding to the above-mentioned generalised geodesic deviation formalism, which include backreaction effects.
Accordingly, the usual geodesic deviation equation \eqref{geodesicdeveq} is replaced by a generalised form that includes these backreaction corrections.  The resulting \emph{generalised} deviation equation governs the true “natural motion” of an extended, backreacting body.

Below, you will find a summary table \ref{tab:GR_frameworks} of the various regimes of motion in GR discussed throughout this work.


