To conclude, by distinguishing the physically realisable \textit{natural motion} defined by generalised geodesic deviation from the approximated \textit{inertial motion} defined by geodesic motion, one achieves a clearer unification of gravity and natural motion in GR, while preserving the physical distinction between gravitational and non‑inertial phenomena.
In essence, based on the separation of the concepts of inertial and natural motion, the \lq{}unification\rq{} should \textit{not} be understood to be between gravity and inertia (see \citealp{LEHMKUHL2014}), understood in the sense of geodesic motion, \textit{but between gravity and natural motion}.

\subsection{A cosmological example for the natural motion}\label{5.3}

Cosmology provides a particularly illuminating context in which to distinguish \emph{natural} from \emph{inertial} (geodesic) motion. 
Unlike terrestrial examples—falling apples, fictitious lifts in \textit{gedankenexperiment}, or objects resting on tables, which introduce complicated frictional and environmental forces—the large-scale universe offers systems whose only significant interaction is gravity.2

Consider dark matter, often modelled as a \emph{dark fluid}, whose constituent particles interact (to very high accuracy) only gravitationally. 
What is the \emph{natural} motion of dark matter?  Clearly, it is determined by gravity, yet it is \emph{not} geodesic in any physically realistic sense. Dark matter forms filaments, halos, aggregates, it is influenced (gravitationally) by the surrounding matter and the (ever-present) gravitational field \citep{Navarro1996,Bertone2018}.\footnote{The nature of dark matter is still debated in physics and philosophy. Although among physicists it is not a successful alternative, some philosophers are studying the MOND (\textit{MOdified Newtonian Dynamics}) proposal, which explains the effects of dark matter not by invoking some form of ‘matter’, but by modifying Newton's law of universal gravitation \citep{Martens2020,Martens2023,Duerr2023DM}.} 
In fact, remember that there is no such thing as 'gravity-free spacetime' out there.

One might object that while it is true that on a galactic scale dark matter aggregates around the visible matter of a galaxy forming a dark halo, it is also true that on cosmological scales dark matter follows the \textit{Hubble flow}, which is the\textit{ geodesic motion} of the homogeneous and isotropic dust fluid of the perfectly homogeneous and isotropic Friedmann–Lemaître–Robertson–Walker (FLRW) model.\footnote{This flow describes the large-scale motion of matter, driven by the expansion of spacetime itself. More formally, Hubble flow is the component of recessional velocity of matter due to the expansion, separating it from peculiar velocities caused by local gravitational interactions \citep{Ryden_2016,bamontithebault}. }

A closer look reveals that this objection assumes an approximation of \textit{perfect} homogeneity and isotropy, which the real universe only approximately satisfies.
\textit{Nothing} in the universe follows the Hubble flow because, as already explained, nothing in the universe can follow a geodesic motion.
\textit{This is a useful fantasy} (see also \cite{Bamonti2024} on this point).

In reality, dark matter—and indeed all matter—\emph{deviates} from perfect geodesic motion because the universe contains inhomogeneities and anisotropies \textit{at all scales}.  Structure formation proceeds through the growth of small \textit{perturbations}, leading to the cosmic web of filaments and voids.  These perturbations break the smooth congruence of FLRW geodesics and induce relative accelerations governed by the geodesic deviation equation (and its generalisations with backreaction) rather than by the geodesic equation alone.\footnote{See \citealp[\S9]{Kolb2018} on the physics of structure formation and the role of dark matter.}

Thus, the “natural motion” of dark matter is its relative motion within the perturbed spacetime, determined by tidal forces and self‑gravitational backreaction.  This example vividly supports the thesis that \emph{natural} motion is governed by geodesic deviation (plus backreaction terms), whereas \emph{inertial} motion, in the strict geodesic sense, remains a useful but ultimately unrealisable idealisation.













\section{Conclusion}

Our journey ends here, for now. On the 2,000-year-old road to inertia, I have explored only a few passages alredy marked by the ideas of Newton and Einstein, and then begun to walk down a hitherto unexplored side lane.

I have led you in a direction where inertial motion and natural motion represent two different concepts, formalised by different equations of motion: geodesic vs. (generalised) geodesic deviation motion, respectively.

This result is not intended to be the end point of the long road to understanding inertia, but the results obtained may be useful in illuminating an uphill road that is difficult to traverse and full of side roads yet to be explored. 

In summary, my investigation reveals that the standard picture of inertial motion—as exemplified by geodesic motion—is, at best, a “useful fantasy” when applied to realistic, extended bodies.
The analysis shows that while the test particle approximation serves as a mathematically tractable tool, it fails to capture the complex backreaction effects and internal structure inherent to real physical systems. Consequently, the longstanding identification of inertial and natural motion needs to be revisited.
By disentangling these concepts and formalising natural motion via generalised deviation equations, I removed the circularity and triviality that afflict conventional definitions of inertia. 
This perspective not only clarifies the status of the Equivalence Principle as an operational approximation, but also suggests new directions for unifying gravity with natural, rather than inertial, motion

Future research might extend this framework to field theories: what does it mean for a field configuration, governed by Euler–Lagrange equations, to “move naturally” rather than “inertially”?  How can we formally distinguish between inertial and natural motion within a field-theoretic framework?  How do backreaction and multipolar structure influence field dynamics?
Addressing these questions could lead to a deeper understanding of the interplay between symmetry and dynamics.

Additionally, it would be intriguing to extend the conceptual framework introduced in this paper to explore its applicability in the context of gravitational wave physics and Newton-Cartan theories and other (non) relativistic alternative theories of gravity \citep{BeltrnJimnez2019,Wolf2024}.

Ultimately, my invitation is to acknowledge the limitations of our traditional concepts and to explore new models that more accurately reflect the nuances of physical reality. By doing so, we can bridge the gap between elegant theoretical constructs and the messy complexity of the real world.


